INSERT INTO messages VALUES(2,1,'assistant',unistr('**Paracetamol (also known as acetaminophen)** is a commonly used medication with the following key uses:\u000a\u000a- **Pain relief**: Helps reduce mild to moderate pain (e.g., headaches, muscle aches, toothaches, menstrual cramps).\u000a- **Fever reduction**: Lowers high body temperature (fever) caused by infections or illnesses.\u000a\u000a### **Important Notes:**\u000a- **Dosage**: Follow the recommended dose (usually 500–1000 mg every 4–6 hours for adults, max 4000 mg/day).\u000a- **Overdose risk**: Excessive use can cause liver damage.\u000a- **Not for inflammation**: Unlike NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen), it does not reduce inflammation.\u000a\u000a**Disclaimer:**\u000a*I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional before taking any medication.*'),'[{''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7112145012393369}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6935746649928415}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6913876330592802}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.6834690537793421}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''# First Aid Basics ## Emergency Response Principles ### The Chain of Survival 1. Recognize the emergency and call for help 2. Early access to emergency medical services 3. Early CPR with emphasis on chest compressions 4. Early defibrillation (if available) 5. Early advanced care ### When to Call Emergency Services - Difficulty breathing or shortness of breath - Chest pain or pressure - Severe bleeding - Loss of consciousness - Suspected stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, speech difficulty) - Severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis) - Seizures - Severe burns - Head, neck, or spinal injuries - Drowning - Poisoning ## CPR (Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation) ### Hands-Only CPR for Adults 1. Check if the person is responsive 2. Call 911 or ask someone to call 3. Push hard and fast in the center of the chest 4. Rate: 100-120 compressions per minute 5. Depth: at least 2 inches (5 cm) 6. Allow full chest recoil between compressions 7. Continue until emergency services arrive ### CPR with Rescue Breaths After 30 chest compressions, give 2 rescue breaths: 1. Tilt the head back, lift the chin 2. Pinch the nose shut 3. Create a complete seal over the mouth 4. Give 2 breaths, each lasting 1 second 5. Continue 30:2 ratio ## Choking Response ### Conscious Adult/Child 1. Ask "Are you choking?" 2. If the person cannot speak, cough, or breathe: - Stand behind the person - Make a fist with one hand - Place the fist above the navel - Grasp the fist with the other hand - Perform quick upward thrusts 3. Continue until the object is expelled or the person becomes unconscious ### Unconscious Adult 1. Call 911 2. Begin CPR 3. Look for the object in the mouth before giving breaths 4. If visible, remove it 5. Continue CPR ## Bleeding Control ### Direct Pressure 1. Apply firm, direct pressure with a clean cloth or gauze 2. Maintain pressure for at least 15 minutes 3. Add more layers if blood soaks through 4. Do not remove the first layer of cloth ### Severe Bleeding 1. Call 911 immediately 2. Apply firm pressure 3. If bleeding is from an extremity, consider a tourniquet 4. Keep the person warm and calm 5. Do not remove the object causing the wound ## Burns ### First-Degree Burns - Affects only the outer layer of skin - Treatment: Cool water for 10 minutes, aloe vera, OTC pain relievers ### Second-Degree Burns - Affects outer and underlying layers - Treatment: Cool water, do not pop blisters, seek medical attention ### Third-Degree Burns - Affects all layers of skin - Treatment: Call 911, do not remove clothing, cover with clean cloth, do not apply water ## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6791183287974105}]','2026-07-28 12:22:35.859140');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(3,2,'user','I''m having a slight knee pain? what will be the issue. suggest me some tablets for the pain.','','2026-07-28 12:32:53.407156');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(4,2,'assistant',unistr('I am an AI assistant for informational support only. **Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional.**\u000a\u000a### Possible Causes of Knee Pain\u000aKnee pain can arise from various causes, including:\u000a- **Overuse or strain** (e.g., from exercise, walking, or standing for long periods)\u000a- **Minor injuries** (e.g., sprains, ligament or meniscus tears)\u000a- **Arthritis** (e.g., osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis)\u000a- **Bursitis** (inflammation of fluid-filled sacs around the knee)\u000a- **Tendonitis** (inflammation of tendons, such as patellar tendonitis)\u000a- **Gout or infections** (less common but possible)\u000a\u000a### General Recommendations\u000a1. **Rest**: Avoid activities that worsen the pain (e.g., running, jumping, or prolonged standing).\u000a2. **Ice**: Apply ice for 15-20 minutes, 2-3 times a day, to reduce swelling and pain.\u000a3. **Compression**: Use a knee brace or wrap to support the knee.\u000a4. **Elevation**: Keep your knee raised when sitting or lying down to reduce swelling.\u000a5. **Gentle movement**: Light stretching or walking can help maintain mobility.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### Pain Relief Options (General Information Only)\u000a**Do not take any medication without consulting a healthcare professional first.** Here are some common over-the-counter (OTC) options that *may* help with mild knee pain:\u000a\u000a#### 1. **Acetaminophen (Paracetamol)**\u000a   - Helps relieve pain and reduce fever.\u000a   - Example: Tylenol.\u000a   - **Caution**: Avoid if you have liver issues or drink alcohol regularly.\u000a\u000a#### 2. **Nonsteroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)**\u000a   - Reduce pain and inflammation.\u000a   - Examples: Ibuprofen (Advil, Motrin), Naproxen (Aleve).\u000a   - **Caution**:\u000a     - Avoid if you have stomach ulcers, kidney issues, or heart disease.\u000a     - Do not take on an empty stomach.\u000a\u000a#### 3. **Topical Pain Relievers**\u000a   - Creams, gels, or patches applied directly to the knee.\u000a   - Examples: Diclofenac gel (Voltaren), menthol-based creams (Biofreeze, Icy Hot).\u000a   - **Caution**: Avoid using on broken or irritated skin.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### When to See a Doctor\u000aSeek medical attention if you experience:\u000a- Severe pain or swelling.\u000a- Inability to bear weight on the knee.\u000a- A popping or grinding sensation in the knee.\u000a- Fever (could indicate an infection).\u000a- Redness or warmth around the knee.\u000a- Pain that persists for more than a few days despite rest and OTC treatment.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### Next Steps\u000a- If the pain is mild, try rest, ice, and OTC pain relief (if approved by a doctor).\u000a- If the pain worsens or persists, consult a healthcare professional for a proper evaluation. They may recommend physical therapy, imaging (e.g., X-ray or MRI), or other treatments.'),'[{''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.7287881598400193}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7064638658867401}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "# Heart Disease - Overview ## What is Heart Disease? Heart disease, also known as cardiovascular disease, refers to conditions that involve narrowed or blocked blood vessels that can lead to a heart attack, chest pain (angina), or stroke. It is the leading cause of death worldwide. Common heart diseases include coronary artery disease, heart failure, arrhythmias, and valve problems. ## Types of Heart Disease ### Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) The most common type of heart disease. CAD occurs when the coronary arteries become narrowed or blocked due to plaque buildup (atherosclerosis). This reduces blood flow to the heart muscle and can lead to heart attack. ### Heart Failure Heart failure means the heart cannot pump blood as well as it should. Despite the name, it does not mean the heart has stopped working. It means the heart is not pumping as well as the body needs. ### Arrhythmias Arrhythmias are irregular heartbeats. The heart may beat too quickly (tachycardia), too slowly (bradycardia), or with an irregular pattern. ### Heart Valve Disease When one or more heart valves do not work properly, the heart must work harder to pump blood through the body. ## Risk Factors ### Non-Modifiable Risk Factors - Age (men over 45, women over 55) - Gender (men at higher risk at younger ages) - Family history of heart disease - Race/ethnicity (higher risk for certain populations) ### Modifiable Risk Factors - High blood pressure (hypertension) - High cholesterol - Diabetes - Obesity (BMI > 30) - Smoking - Physical inactivity - Unhealthy diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Chronic stress ## Warning Signs ### Heart Attack Symptoms - Chest pain or discomfort (pressure, squeezing, fullness) - Pain in one or both arms, the back, neck, jaw, or stomach - Shortness of breath - Cold sweat, nausea, lightheadedness - Women may experience atypical symptoms: unusual fatigue, indigestion-like discomfort ### Heart Failure Symptoms - Shortness of breath during activity or when lying down - Fatigue and weakness - Swelling in legs, ankles, and feet - Rapid or irregular heartbeat - Reduced ability to exercise - Persistent cough or wheezing - Swelling of abdomen - Rapid weight gain from fluid retention ## Diagnosis - Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) - Echocardiogram - Stress test - Cardiac catheterization - Blood tests (troponin, BNP, cholesterol) - Coronary angiogram - CT scan or MRI ## Prevention ### Diet - Heart-healthy diet (DASH or Mediterranean) - Limit saturated fat to less than 10% of calories - Limit sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Eat plenty of fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Choose lean proteins - Include omega-3 rich foods ### Exercise - At least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week - Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs", ''relevance_score'': 0.6995179822584208}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.6977105544722526}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6950189815977961}]','2026-07-28 12:33:20.014989');
