INSERT INTO messages VALUES(10,4,'assistant',unistr('It looks like your message got cut off. How can I assist you today? Are you looking for information on a specific health topic, first aid guidance, or nutrition advice?\u000a\u000a**I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional.**'),'[{''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6843948446130861}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': "# Nutrition and Healthy Eating Guide ## Macronutrients ### Carbohydrates Carbohydrates are the body''s main source of energy. They are found in foods like fruits, vegetables, grains, and legumes. - Recommended intake: 45-65% of total daily calories - Complex carbohydrates (whole grains, vegetables) are preferred over simple sugars - Dietary fiber (25-30 grams per day) is essential for digestive health - Limit added sugars to less than 10% of daily calories ### Proteins Proteins are essential for building and repairing tissues, making enzymes and hormones, and supporting immune function. - Recommended intake: 10-35% of total daily calories - Good sources: lean meats, fish, eggs, dairy, beans, nuts, tofu - Complete proteins contain all essential amino acids - Plant-based proteins (beans, lentils, nuts) are heart-healthy options ### Fats Fats are necessary for nutrient absorption, hormone production, and brain health. - Recommended intake: 20-35% of total daily calories - Unsaturated fats (olive oil, avocados, nuts) are healthier choices - Limit saturated fats to less than 10% of daily calories - Avoid trans fats completely - Omega-3 fatty acids (fish, flaxseed, walnuts) support heart health ## Micronutrients ### Essential Vitamins - Vitamin A: Vision, immune function (carrots, sweet potatoes, spinach) - Vitamin B12: Nerve function, red blood cells (meat, fish, dairy) - Vitamin C: Immune function, skin health (citrus fruits, peppers, berries) - Vitamin D: Bone health, immune function (sunlight, fatty fish, fortified foods) - Vitamin K: Blood clotting, bone health (leafy greens, broccoli) ### Essential Minerals - Calcium: Bone health (dairy, leafy greens, fortified foods) - Iron: Oxygen transport (red meat, spinach, beans) - Potassium: Heart function, blood pressure (bananas, potatoes, avocados) - Zinc: Immune function, wound healing (meat, seeds, legumes) ## Hydration ### Water Intake - General recommendation: 8-10 glasses (2-2.5 liters) per day - Needs increase with exercise, heat, illness, or pregnancy - Signs of dehydration: dark urine, dry mouth, fatigue, headache - Water is the best choice for hydration - Limit sugary drinks and excessive caffeine ### Beverages to Limit - Sugary sodas and energy drinks - Fruit juices with added sugar - Excessive alcohol (1 drink/day women, 2 drinks/day men) - High-caffeine beverages ## Healthy Eating Patterns ### Mediterranean Diet - Rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts - Olive oil as primary fat source - Moderate fish and poultry - Limited red meat - Associated with reduced heart disease, diabetes, and cancer risk ### DASH Diet - Designed to lower blood pressure - High in fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Low in sodium, red meat, added sugars - Rich in potassium, calcium, magnesium ## Dietary Tips for Specific Conditions ### For Diabetes - Monitor carbohydrate intake - Choose low glycemic index foods - Eat regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat", ''relevance_score'': 0.6784001086621981}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''# First Aid Basics ## Emergency Response Principles ### The Chain of Survival 1. Recognize the emergency and call for help 2. Early access to emergency medical services 3. Early CPR with emphasis on chest compressions 4. Early defibrillation (if available) 5. Early advanced care ### When to Call Emergency Services - Difficulty breathing or shortness of breath - Chest pain or pressure - Severe bleeding - Loss of consciousness - Suspected stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, speech difficulty) - Severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis) - Seizures - Severe burns - Head, neck, or spinal injuries - Drowning - Poisoning ## CPR (Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation) ### Hands-Only CPR for Adults 1. Check if the person is responsive 2. Call 911 or ask someone to call 3. Push hard and fast in the center of the chest 4. Rate: 100-120 compressions per minute 5. Depth: at least 2 inches (5 cm) 6. Allow full chest recoil between compressions 7. Continue until emergency services arrive ### CPR with Rescue Breaths After 30 chest compressions, give 2 rescue breaths: 1. Tilt the head back, lift the chin 2. Pinch the nose shut 3. Create a complete seal over the mouth 4. Give 2 breaths, each lasting 1 second 5. Continue 30:2 ratio ## Choking Response ### Conscious Adult/Child 1. Ask "Are you choking?" 2. If the person cannot speak, cough, or breathe: - Stand behind the person - Make a fist with one hand - Place the fist above the navel - Grasp the fist with the other hand - Perform quick upward thrusts 3. Continue until the object is expelled or the person becomes unconscious ### Unconscious Adult 1. Call 911 2. Begin CPR 3. Look for the object in the mouth before giving breaths 4. If visible, remove it 5. Continue CPR ## Bleeding Control ### Direct Pressure 1. Apply firm, direct pressure with a clean cloth or gauze 2. Maintain pressure for at least 15 minutes 3. Add more layers if blood soaks through 4. Do not remove the first layer of cloth ### Severe Bleeding 1. Call 911 immediately 2. Apply firm pressure 3. If bleeding is from an extremity, consider a tourniquet 4. Keep the person warm and calm 5. Do not remove the object causing the wound ## Burns ### First-Degree Burns - Affects only the outer layer of skin - Treatment: Cool water for 10 minutes, aloe vera, OTC pain relievers ### Second-Degree Burns - Affects outer and underlying layers - Treatment: Cool water, do not pop blisters, seek medical attention ### Third-Degree Burns - Affects all layers of skin - Treatment: Call 911, do not remove clothing, cover with clean cloth, do not apply water ## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6701499091364149}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6646872166046086}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "# Heart Disease - Overview ## What is Heart Disease? Heart disease, also known as cardiovascular disease, refers to conditions that involve narrowed or blocked blood vessels that can lead to a heart attack, chest pain (angina), or stroke. It is the leading cause of death worldwide. Common heart diseases include coronary artery disease, heart failure, arrhythmias, and valve problems. ## Types of Heart Disease ### Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) The most common type of heart disease. CAD occurs when the coronary arteries become narrowed or blocked due to plaque buildup (atherosclerosis). This reduces blood flow to the heart muscle and can lead to heart attack. ### Heart Failure Heart failure means the heart cannot pump blood as well as it should. Despite the name, it does not mean the heart has stopped working. It means the heart is not pumping as well as the body needs. ### Arrhythmias Arrhythmias are irregular heartbeats. The heart may beat too quickly (tachycardia), too slowly (bradycardia), or with an irregular pattern. ### Heart Valve Disease When one or more heart valves do not work properly, the heart must work harder to pump blood through the body. ## Risk Factors ### Non-Modifiable Risk Factors - Age (men over 45, women over 55) - Gender (men at higher risk at younger ages) - Family history of heart disease - Race/ethnicity (higher risk for certain populations) ### Modifiable Risk Factors - High blood pressure (hypertension) - High cholesterol - Diabetes - Obesity (BMI > 30) - Smoking - Physical inactivity - Unhealthy diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Chronic stress ## Warning Signs ### Heart Attack Symptoms - Chest pain or discomfort (pressure, squeezing, fullness) - Pain in one or both arms, the back, neck, jaw, or stomach - Shortness of breath - Cold sweat, nausea, lightheadedness - Women may experience atypical symptoms: unusual fatigue, indigestion-like discomfort ### Heart Failure Symptoms - Shortness of breath during activity or when lying down - Fatigue and weakness - Swelling in legs, ankles, and feet - Rapid or irregular heartbeat - Reduced ability to exercise - Persistent cough or wheezing - Swelling of abdomen - Rapid weight gain from fluid retention ## Diagnosis - Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) - Echocardiogram - Stress test - Cardiac catheterization - Blood tests (troponin, BNP, cholesterol) - Coronary angiogram - CT scan or MRI ## Prevention ### Diet - Heart-healthy diet (DASH or Mediterranean) - Limit saturated fat to less than 10% of calories - Limit sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Eat plenty of fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Choose lean proteins - Include omega-3 rich foods ### Exercise - At least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week - Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs", ''relevance_score'': 0.6587825943659253}]','2026-07-28 12:54:04.041991');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(11,4,'user','I am 20 years old and i am skinny fat','','2026-07-28 12:54:33.666656');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(12,4,'assistant',unistr('Thank you for sharing that with me. The term "skinny fat" (or metabolically obese normal weight) describes someone who may appear thin but has a higher body fat percentage and lower muscle mass, which can still pose health risks. Here’s what you should know:\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Understanding "Skinny Fat"**\u000a- **Body Composition Matters**: Even if your weight or BMI falls in the "normal" range, a high body fat percentage (especially visceral fat around organs) can increase risks for:\u000a  - Insulin resistance (prediabetes)\u000a  - High cholesterol\u000a  - Heart disease\u000a  - Low energy or fatigue\u000a- **Muscle Mass**: Low muscle tone can weaken metabolism and strength.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Key Steps to Improve Body Composition**\u000a#### **1. Nutrition**\u000a- **Prioritize Protein**: Aim for **1.2–2.2g of protein per kg of body weight** daily (e.g., chicken, fish, eggs, tofu, Greek yogurt) to build muscle.\u000a- **Healthy Fats**: Include avocados, nuts, olive oil, and fatty fish (salmon) for hormone balance and satiety.\u000a- **Complex Carbs**: Choose whole grains (oats, quinoa), vegetables, and fruits for sustained energy.\u000a- **Limit Processed Foods**: Reduce sugary snacks, refined carbs (white bread, pastries), and fried foods.\u000a- **Caloric Surplus (if needed)**: If you’re underweight, eat slightly more calories than you burn to support muscle growth.\u000a\u000a#### **2. Exercise**\u000a- **Strength Training**: Focus on **progressive overload** (gradually increasing weights) 3–4x/week. Compound movements like squats, deadlifts, push-ups, and pull-ups are most effective.\u000a- **Cardio**: Add **2–3 sessions of moderate cardio** (walking, cycling, swimming) per week to improve heart health without losing muscle.\u000a- **Consistency**: Muscle growth takes time—stick with a routine for at least 3–6 months.\u000a\u000a#### **3. Lifestyle**\u000a- **Sleep**: Aim for **7–9 hours/night** to support recovery and hormone regulation (e.g., testosterone, growth hormone).\u000a- **Stress Management**: Chronic stress raises cortisol, which can increase fat storage. Try meditation, deep breathing, or hobbies.\u000a- **Hydration**: Drink **2–3L of water daily** to optimize metabolism and performance.\u000a\u000a#### **4. Monitor Progress**\u000a- **Body Measurements**: Track waist circumference (high visceral fat if >35 inches for women or >40 inches for men) and progress photos.\u000a- **Strength Gains**: Note improvements in lifting weights or endurance.\u000a- **Energy Levels**: Better sleep, digestion, and mood are signs of progress.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **When to See a Professional**\u000a- If you have **unexplained fatigue, irregular periods (for women), or frequent illnesses**, consult a doctor to rule out underlying conditions (e.g., thyroid issues, insulin resistance).\u000a- A **registered dietitian** can help tailor a meal plan to your goals.\u000a- A **personal trainer** can design a safe, effective workout plan.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Sample Routine**\u000a| **Day**       | **Workout**                          | **Notes**                          |\u000a|---------------|--------------------------------------|------------------------------------|\u000a| Monday        | Full-body strength (squats, push-ups)| 3 sets of 8–12 reps                |\u000a| Tuesday       | 30-min brisk walk + core exercises   | Planks, leg raises                 |\u000a| Wednesday     | Upper-body strength (rows, pull-ups) | Focus on form                      |\u000a| Thursday      | Rest or light yoga                   | Stretching, mobility work          |\u000a| Friday        | Lower-body strength (deadlifts, lunges)| 3 sets of 8–12 reps            |\u000a| Saturday      | Cardio (cycling, swimming)           | 45–60 minutes                      |\u000a| Sunday        | Rest                                 | Active recovery (walking, stretching)|\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a**Remember**: Health isn’t just about appearance—it’s about strength, energy, and longevity. Small, sustainable changes work best. If you’d like, I can provide more details on any of these areas!\u000a\u000a**I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional.**'),'[{''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6195317439780249}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': "# Nutrition and Healthy Eating Guide ## Macronutrients ### Carbohydrates Carbohydrates are the body''s main source of energy. They are found in foods like fruits, vegetables, grains, and legumes. - Recommended intake: 45-65% of total daily calories - Complex carbohydrates (whole grains, vegetables) are preferred over simple sugars - Dietary fiber (25-30 grams per day) is essential for digestive health - Limit added sugars to less than 10% of daily calories ### Proteins Proteins are essential for building and repairing tissues, making enzymes and hormones, and supporting immune function. - Recommended intake: 10-35% of total daily calories - Good sources: lean meats, fish, eggs, dairy, beans, nuts, tofu - Complete proteins contain all essential amino acids - Plant-based proteins (beans, lentils, nuts) are heart-healthy options ### Fats Fats are necessary for nutrient absorption, hormone production, and brain health. - Recommended intake: 20-35% of total daily calories - Unsaturated fats (olive oil, avocados, nuts) are healthier choices - Limit saturated fats to less than 10% of daily calories - Avoid trans fats completely - Omega-3 fatty acids (fish, flaxseed, walnuts) support heart health ## Micronutrients ### Essential Vitamins - Vitamin A: Vision, immune function (carrots, sweet potatoes, spinach) - Vitamin B12: Nerve function, red blood cells (meat, fish, dairy) - Vitamin C: Immune function, skin health (citrus fruits, peppers, berries) - Vitamin D: Bone health, immune function (sunlight, fatty fish, fortified foods) - Vitamin K: Blood clotting, bone health (leafy greens, broccoli) ### Essential Minerals - Calcium: Bone health (dairy, leafy greens, fortified foods) - Iron: Oxygen transport (red meat, spinach, beans) - Potassium: Heart function, blood pressure (bananas, potatoes, avocados) - Zinc: Immune function, wound healing (meat, seeds, legumes) ## Hydration ### Water Intake - General recommendation: 8-10 glasses (2-2.5 liters) per day - Needs increase with exercise, heat, illness, or pregnancy - Signs of dehydration: dark urine, dry mouth, fatigue, headache - Water is the best choice for hydration - Limit sugary drinks and excessive caffeine ### Beverages to Limit - Sugary sodas and energy drinks - Fruit juices with added sugar - Excessive alcohol (1 drink/day women, 2 drinks/day men) - High-caffeine beverages ## Healthy Eating Patterns ### Mediterranean Diet - Rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts - Olive oil as primary fat source - Moderate fish and poultry - Limited red meat - Associated with reduced heart disease, diabetes, and cancer risk ### DASH Diet - Designed to lower blood pressure - High in fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Low in sodium, red meat, added sugars - Rich in potassium, calcium, magnesium ## Dietary Tips for Specific Conditions ### For Diabetes - Monitor carbohydrate intake - Choose low glycemic index foods - Eat regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat", ''relevance_score'': 0.6155454338717393}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': ''regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat mindfully and slowly - Include protein at every meal - Eat plenty of fiber-rich foods - Limit processed and high-calorie foods'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6107514870520672}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "# Diabetes Mellitus - Overview ## What is Diabetes? Diabetes mellitus is a chronic metabolic disorder characterized by elevated levels of blood glucose (or blood sugar), which leads over time to serious damage to the heart, blood vessels, eyes, kidneys, and nerves. The most common type is type 2 diabetes, usually in adults, which occurs when the body becomes resistant to insulin or doesn''t make enough insulin. In the past three decades the prevalence of type 2 diabetes has risen dramatically in countries of all income levels. ## Types of Diabetes ### Type 1 Diabetes Type 1 diabetes (formerly known as insulin-dependent or childhood-onset diabetes) is characterized by a lack of insulin production and is caused by the autoimmune destruction of insulin-producing beta cells in the pancreas. The cause of type 1 diabetes is not known and it is not preventable with current knowledge. ### Type 2 Diabetes Type 2 diabetes (formerly called non-insulin-dependent or adult-onset diabetes) results from the body''s ineffective use of insulin. More than 95% of people with diabetes have type 2 diabetes. This type of diabetes is increasingly being diagnosed in more children and adolescents. ### Gestational Diabetes Gestational diabetes is hyperglycemia with first recognition during pregnancy. The main symptoms of gestational diabetes are increased urination, increased thirst, and fatigue. ## Symptoms of Diabetes - Increased thirst (polydipsia) - Increased urination (polyuria) - Increased hunger (polyphagia) - Unexplained weight loss - Fatigue - Blurred vision - Slow-healing sores - Frequent infections - Areas of darkened skin - Numbness or tingling in hands or feet ## Risk Factors for Type 2 Diabetes - Being overweight or obese - Being 45 or older - Having a parent or sibling with type 2 diabetes - Being physically active less than 3 times a week - Having prediabetes - Having had gestational diabetes during pregnancy - Having polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) ## Management ### Diet A balanced diet is essential for managing diabetes. Key dietary recommendations include: - Monitoring carbohydrate intake and choosing complex carbohydrates - Eating plenty of fiber (vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes) - Choosing healthy fats (olive oil, nuts, avocados) - Limiting processed foods and added sugars - Eating regular meals and not skipping breakfast - Controlling portion sizes ### Exercise Regular physical activity helps control blood sugar levels and improves insulin sensitivity. Recommendations: - At least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week - Strength training exercises 2 or more days per week - Reduce prolonged sitting by getting up every 30 minutes ### Blood Sugar Monitoring Regular monitoring of blood glucose levels is essential for diabetes management. The American Diabetes Association recommends: - Fasting blood glucose target: 80-130 mg/dL - Post-meal blood glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation)", ''relevance_score'': 0.5970540011353944}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.5896110111491968}]','2026-07-28 12:54:49.200981');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(13,4,'user','I still didn''t develop a mustache','','2026-07-28 12:54:56.294975');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(14,4,'assistant',unistr('It’s completely normal to still be developing facial hair at 20—everyone’s body grows at its own pace. Here’s what you should know about facial hair growth and how to support healthy development:\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Why Some Men Develop Facial Hair Later**\u000a1. **Genetics**\u000a   - The biggest factor! If your father, uncles, or grandfathers had late or sparse facial hair, you may follow a similar pattern.\u000a   - Ethnic background also plays a role (e.g., men of East Asian or Native American descent often have less facial hair).\u000a\u000a2. **Hormones**\u000a   - **Testosterone** and **DHT (dihydrotestosterone)** drive facial hair growth. Levels peak in your early 20s but can vary widely.\u000a   - **Sensitivity to hormones**: Even with normal testosterone, your hair follicles may be less responsive to it.\u000a\u000a3. **Age**\u000a   - Facial hair often fills in between **ages 18–25**, but some men continue developing into their late 20s or even 30s.\u000a\u000a4. **Nutrition and Health**\u000a   - **Deficiencies** (e.g., zinc, vitamin D, iron) can delay hair growth.\u000a   - **Chronic stress** or poor sleep may disrupt hormone balance.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **How to Support Healthy Facial Hair Growth**\u000a#### **1. Nutrition**\u000a- **Protein**: Hair is made of keratin (a protein). Eat eggs, lean meats, beans, and nuts.\u000a- **Zinc**: Supports testosterone and hair growth (oysters, pumpkin seeds, lentils).\u000a- **Vitamin D**: Low levels are linked to hair loss (sunlight, fatty fish, fortified milk).\u000a- **Biotin**: A B-vitamin that may help (eggs, almonds, sweet potatoes).\u000a- **Healthy fats**: Omega-3s (salmon, walnuts) reduce inflammation.\u000a\u000a#### **2. Lifestyle**\u000a- **Sleep**: Aim for **7–9 hours/night** to optimize testosterone.\u000a- **Exercise**: Strength training (especially compound lifts like squats) boosts testosterone naturally.\u000a- **Stress management**: High cortisol can suppress testosterone. Try meditation, walking, or hobbies.\u000a- **Avoid smoking/alcohol**: Both can interfere with hormone balance and hair growth.\u000a\u000a#### **3. Skincare for Facial Hair**\u000a- **Exfoliate**: Gently scrub your face 1–2x/week to remove dead skin and unclog follicles.\u000a- **Moisturize**: Use a light, non-comedogenic moisturizer to keep skin healthy.\u000a- **Be patient with shaving**: Shaving doesn’t make hair grow back thicker, but it can help you track progress.\u000a\u000a#### **4. Medical Considerations**\u000a- **Rule out deficiencies**: Ask your doctor about testing for **zinc, vitamin D, or iron** if you’re concerned.\u000a- **Hormone levels**: If you have other symptoms (e.g., low energy, muscle loss, low libido), a doctor can check testosterone levels.\u000a- **Topical minoxidil (Rogaine)**: Some men use it off-label to stimulate facial hair growth (consult a dermatologist first).\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **What NOT to Do**\u000a- **Don’t compare yourself to others**: Social media and peers can create unrealistic expectations. Genetics are the biggest factor.\u000a- **Avoid "miracle" supplements**: Many products (e.g., biotin gummies, "beard oils") lack scientific backing. Focus on nutrition and patience.\u000a- **Don’t stress**: Anxiety about facial hair can actually delay growth by raising cortisol.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **When to See a Doctor**\u000aConsult a healthcare provider if you notice:\u000a- **No facial hair by age 25–30** (especially if other body hair is sparse).\u000a- **Other symptoms** like fatigue, weight gain, or loss of muscle mass (could indicate hormonal issues).\u000a- **Patchy or sudden hair loss** (could signal alopecia or other conditions).\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Realistic Timeline**\u000a- **Ages 15–18**: Light, patchy hair may appear.\u000a- **Ages 18–25**: Hair thickens and fills in for most men.\u000a- **Ages 25–30+**: Some men continue to develop denser facial hair.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Emotional Support**\u000aIt’s okay to feel frustrated—society often ties masculinity to facial hair. But remember:\u000a- **Confidence comes from within**: Focus on your strengths (e.g., fitness, skills, personality).\u000a- **Grooming alternatives**: If you prefer a clean-shaven look, own it! Many men look great without facial hair.\u000a- **Community**: Online forums (e.g., Reddit’s r/beards or r/skincareaddiction) can offer'),'[{''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.5871449104181261}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.5685778337137645}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.5582282354943617}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "# Diabetes Mellitus - Overview ## What is Diabetes? Diabetes mellitus is a chronic metabolic disorder characterized by elevated levels of blood glucose (or blood sugar), which leads over time to serious damage to the heart, blood vessels, eyes, kidneys, and nerves. The most common type is type 2 diabetes, usually in adults, which occurs when the body becomes resistant to insulin or doesn''t make enough insulin. In the past three decades the prevalence of type 2 diabetes has risen dramatically in countries of all income levels. ## Types of Diabetes ### Type 1 Diabetes Type 1 diabetes (formerly known as insulin-dependent or childhood-onset diabetes) is characterized by a lack of insulin production and is caused by the autoimmune destruction of insulin-producing beta cells in the pancreas. The cause of type 1 diabetes is not known and it is not preventable with current knowledge. ### Type 2 Diabetes Type 2 diabetes (formerly called non-insulin-dependent or adult-onset diabetes) results from the body''s ineffective use of insulin. More than 95% of people with diabetes have type 2 diabetes. This type of diabetes is increasingly being diagnosed in more children and adolescents. ### Gestational Diabetes Gestational diabetes is hyperglycemia with first recognition during pregnancy. The main symptoms of gestational diabetes are increased urination, increased thirst, and fatigue. ## Symptoms of Diabetes - Increased thirst (polydipsia) - Increased urination (polyuria) - Increased hunger (polyphagia) - Unexplained weight loss - Fatigue - Blurred vision - Slow-healing sores - Frequent infections - Areas of darkened skin - Numbness or tingling in hands or feet ## Risk Factors for Type 2 Diabetes - Being overweight or obese - Being 45 or older - Having a parent or sibling with type 2 diabetes - Being physically active less than 3 times a week - Having prediabetes - Having had gestational diabetes during pregnancy - Having polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) ## Management ### Diet A balanced diet is essential for managing diabetes. Key dietary recommendations include: - Monitoring carbohydrate intake and choosing complex carbohydrates - Eating plenty of fiber (vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes) - Choosing healthy fats (olive oil, nuts, avocados) - Limiting processed foods and added sugars - Eating regular meals and not skipping breakfast - Controlling portion sizes ### Exercise Regular physical activity helps control blood sugar levels and improves insulin sensitivity. Recommendations: - At least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week - Strength training exercises 2 or more days per week - Reduce prolonged sitting by getting up every 30 minutes ### Blood Sugar Monitoring Regular monitoring of blood glucose levels is essential for diabetes management. The American Diabetes Association recommends: - Fasting blood glucose target: 80-130 mg/dL - Post-meal blood glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation)", ''relevance_score'': 0.5519546210607215}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': "# Nutrition and Healthy Eating Guide ## Macronutrients ### Carbohydrates Carbohydrates are the body''s main source of energy. They are found in foods like fruits, vegetables, grains, and legumes. - Recommended intake: 45-65% of total daily calories - Complex carbohydrates (whole grains, vegetables) are preferred over simple sugars - Dietary fiber (25-30 grams per day) is essential for digestive health - Limit added sugars to less than 10% of daily calories ### Proteins Proteins are essential for building and repairing tissues, making enzymes and hormones, and supporting immune function. - Recommended intake: 10-35% of total daily calories - Good sources: lean meats, fish, eggs, dairy, beans, nuts, tofu - Complete proteins contain all essential amino acids - Plant-based proteins (beans, lentils, nuts) are heart-healthy options ### Fats Fats are necessary for nutrient absorption, hormone production, and brain health. - Recommended intake: 20-35% of total daily calories - Unsaturated fats (olive oil, avocados, nuts) are healthier choices - Limit saturated fats to less than 10% of daily calories - Avoid trans fats completely - Omega-3 fatty acids (fish, flaxseed, walnuts) support heart health ## Micronutrients ### Essential Vitamins - Vitamin A: Vision, immune function (carrots, sweet potatoes, spinach) - Vitamin B12: Nerve function, red blood cells (meat, fish, dairy) - Vitamin C: Immune function, skin health (citrus fruits, peppers, berries) - Vitamin D: Bone health, immune function (sunlight, fatty fish, fortified foods) - Vitamin K: Blood clotting, bone health (leafy greens, broccoli) ### Essential Minerals - Calcium: Bone health (dairy, leafy greens, fortified foods) - Iron: Oxygen transport (red meat, spinach, beans) - Potassium: Heart function, blood pressure (bananas, potatoes, avocados) - Zinc: Immune function, wound healing (meat, seeds, legumes) ## Hydration ### Water Intake - General recommendation: 8-10 glasses (2-2.5 liters) per day - Needs increase with exercise, heat, illness, or pregnancy - Signs of dehydration: dark urine, dry mouth, fatigue, headache - Water is the best choice for hydration - Limit sugary drinks and excessive caffeine ### Beverages to Limit - Sugary sodas and energy drinks - Fruit juices with added sugar - Excessive alcohol (1 drink/day women, 2 drinks/day men) - High-caffeine beverages ## Healthy Eating Patterns ### Mediterranean Diet - Rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts - Olive oil as primary fat source - Moderate fish and poultry - Limited red meat - Associated with reduced heart disease, diabetes, and cancer risk ### DASH Diet - Designed to lower blood pressure - High in fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Low in sodium, red meat, added sugars - Rich in potassium, calcium, magnesium ## Dietary Tips for Specific Conditions ### For Diabetes - Monitor carbohydrate intake - Choose low glycemic index foods - Eat regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat", ''relevance_score'': 0.5497082089896133}]','2026-07-28 12:55:14.087199');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(15,5,'user','What to eat for a sore throat?','','2026-07-28 12:56:51.006839');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(16,5,'assistant',unistr('When you have a sore throat, it''s important to choose foods that are soothing, easy to swallow, and nutritious. Here are some recommendations based on general medical knowledge:\u000a\u000a### **Soothing Foods and Drinks**\u000a1. **Warm Liquids**\u000a   - Herbal teas (chamomile, ginger, licorice root)\u000a   - Warm water with honey (for adults and children over 1 year)\u000a   - Broths and soups (chicken soup can be particularly soothing)\u000a\u000a2. **Cold Foods**\u000a   - Ice chips or popsicles (can help numb the throat)\u000a   - Cold yogurt or smoothies (avoid citrus if it causes irritation)\u000a\u000a3. **Soft Foods**\u000a   - Mashed potatoes\u000a   - Oatmeal or porridge\u000a   - Scrambled eggs\u000a   - Applesauce\u000a   - Pudding or custard\u000a\u000a4. **Hydrating Foods**\u000a   - Watermelon\u000a   - Cucumber\u000a   - Cooked vegetables (like carrots or squash)\u000a\u000a### **Foods to Avoid**\u000a- **Spicy foods** (can irritate the throat)\u000a- **Acidic foods** (citrus fruits, tomatoes, vinegar)\u000a- **Crunchy or hard foods** (chips, crackers, raw vegetables)\u000a- **Very hot foods or drinks** (can cause further irritation)\u000a\u000a### **Additional Tips**\u000a- **Gargle with warm salt water** (1/2 tsp salt in 1 cup warm water) to reduce inflammation.\u000a- **Stay hydrated** to keep the throat moist.\u000a- **Avoid caffeine and alcohol**, as they can dehydrate you.\u000a\u000a---\u000a**I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional if your sore throat persists or worsens.**'),'[{''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': "# Nutrition and Healthy Eating Guide ## Macronutrients ### Carbohydrates Carbohydrates are the body''s main source of energy. They are found in foods like fruits, vegetables, grains, and legumes. - Recommended intake: 45-65% of total daily calories - Complex carbohydrates (whole grains, vegetables) are preferred over simple sugars - Dietary fiber (25-30 grams per day) is essential for digestive health - Limit added sugars to less than 10% of daily calories ### Proteins Proteins are essential for building and repairing tissues, making enzymes and hormones, and supporting immune function. - Recommended intake: 10-35% of total daily calories - Good sources: lean meats, fish, eggs, dairy, beans, nuts, tofu - Complete proteins contain all essential amino acids - Plant-based proteins (beans, lentils, nuts) are heart-healthy options ### Fats Fats are necessary for nutrient absorption, hormone production, and brain health. - Recommended intake: 20-35% of total daily calories - Unsaturated fats (olive oil, avocados, nuts) are healthier choices - Limit saturated fats to less than 10% of daily calories - Avoid trans fats completely - Omega-3 fatty acids (fish, flaxseed, walnuts) support heart health ## Micronutrients ### Essential Vitamins - Vitamin A: Vision, immune function (carrots, sweet potatoes, spinach) - Vitamin B12: Nerve function, red blood cells (meat, fish, dairy) - Vitamin C: Immune function, skin health (citrus fruits, peppers, berries) - Vitamin D: Bone health, immune function (sunlight, fatty fish, fortified foods) - Vitamin K: Blood clotting, bone health (leafy greens, broccoli) ### Essential Minerals - Calcium: Bone health (dairy, leafy greens, fortified foods) - Iron: Oxygen transport (red meat, spinach, beans) - Potassium: Heart function, blood pressure (bananas, potatoes, avocados) - Zinc: Immune function, wound healing (meat, seeds, legumes) ## Hydration ### Water Intake - General recommendation: 8-10 glasses (2-2.5 liters) per day - Needs increase with exercise, heat, illness, or pregnancy - Signs of dehydration: dark urine, dry mouth, fatigue, headache - Water is the best choice for hydration - Limit sugary drinks and excessive caffeine ### Beverages to Limit - Sugary sodas and energy drinks - Fruit juices with added sugar - Excessive alcohol (1 drink/day women, 2 drinks/day men) - High-caffeine beverages ## Healthy Eating Patterns ### Mediterranean Diet - Rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts - Olive oil as primary fat source - Moderate fish and poultry - Limited red meat - Associated with reduced heart disease, diabetes, and cancer risk ### DASH Diet - Designed to lower blood pressure - High in fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Low in sodium, red meat, added sugars - Rich in potassium, calcium, magnesium ## Dietary Tips for Specific Conditions ### For Diabetes - Monitor carbohydrate intake - Choose low glycemic index foods - Eat regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat", ''relevance_score'': 0.7190928372359918}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': "# Fever, Cold, and Influenza (Flu) ## Fever ### What is Fever? Fever is an increase in the body''s temperature, which is regulated by the hypothalamus. A normal body temperature is approximately 98.6°F (37°C). A fever is generally considered to be a temperature of 100.4°F (38°C) or higher. ### Causes of Fever - Infections (bacterial, viral, fungal) - Inflammatory conditions - Medications - Heat stroke - Certain cancers - Autoimmune disorders ### When to See a Doctor - Infants under 3 months: any fever over 100.4°F (38°C) - Children 3 months to 3 years: fever of 102.2°F (39°C) or higher - Adults: fever above 103°F (39.4°C) or fever lasting more than 3 days - Anyone with fever accompanied by severe headache, stiff neck, confusion, or rash ### Fever Management - Stay hydrated by drinking plenty of fluids - Rest adequately - Dress in lightweight clothing - Take acetaminophen or ibuprofen (as directed) - Lukewarm sponge bath if temperature is very high - Monitor temperature regularly ## Common Cold ### What is a Cold? The common cold is a viral infection of your nose and throat (upper respiratory tract). It is usually harmless, although it might not feel that way. Many types of viruses can cause a cold, but rhinoviruses are the most common. ### Symptoms - Runny or stuffy nose - Sore throat - Cough - Congestion - Slight body aches - Sneezing - Low-grade fever - Generally feeling unwell (malaise) ### Duration - Onset: 1-3 days after exposure - Duration: 7-10 days - Cough may persist for 2 weeks ### Treatment - Rest - Stay hydrated - Saline nasal drops or spray - Warm liquids (tea, broth) - Honey (for adults and children over 1 year) - Over-the-counter cold medications (for adults) - Gargle with warm salt water for sore throat ## Influenza (Flu) ### What is the Flu? Influenza (flu) is a contagious respiratory illness caused by influenza viruses. It can cause mild to severe illness, and at times can lead to death. Flu is different from a cold. ### Flu vs Cold The flu and the common cold have similar symptoms, but the flu is generally more severe: - Flu: sudden onset, high fever (100-104°F), severe body aches, extreme fatigue - Cold: gradual onset, low or no fever, mild aches, mild fatigue ### Flu Symptoms - Fever (usually high) - Severe body aches - Headache - Fatigue (can last 2-3 weeks) - Dry cough - Sore throat - Nasal congestion - Chills - Sweating ### When to Seek Emergency Care - Difficulty breathing or shortness of breath - Persistent pain or pressure in chest - Sudden dizziness or confusion - Severe or persistent vomiting - Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours -", ''relevance_score'': 0.702913237087465}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6922115459575383}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.689603294835253}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': ''regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat mindfully and slowly - Include protein at every meal - Eat plenty of fiber-rich foods - Limit processed and high-calorie foods'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6883557398816106}]','2026-07-28 12:56:57.845936');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(17,1,'user','tell me about cars. in 2 lines','','2026-07-28 13:14:55.240642');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(18,1,'assistant','I am an AI Healthcare Assistant, so I can only answer questions related to health, medicine, and wellness. Your query about cars is not related to the medical field. Please feel free to ask any medical or health-related question!','[{''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6009462876133621}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.5899362114822384}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.5894478820150538}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "# Heart Disease - Overview ## What is Heart Disease? Heart disease, also known as cardiovascular disease, refers to conditions that involve narrowed or blocked blood vessels that can lead to a heart attack, chest pain (angina), or stroke. It is the leading cause of death worldwide. Common heart diseases include coronary artery disease, heart failure, arrhythmias, and valve problems. ## Types of Heart Disease ### Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) The most common type of heart disease. CAD occurs when the coronary arteries become narrowed or blocked due to plaque buildup (atherosclerosis). This reduces blood flow to the heart muscle and can lead to heart attack. ### Heart Failure Heart failure means the heart cannot pump blood as well as it should. Despite the name, it does not mean the heart has stopped working. It means the heart is not pumping as well as the body needs. ### Arrhythmias Arrhythmias are irregular heartbeats. The heart may beat too quickly (tachycardia), too slowly (bradycardia), or with an irregular pattern. ### Heart Valve Disease When one or more heart valves do not work properly, the heart must work harder to pump blood through the body. ## Risk Factors ### Non-Modifiable Risk Factors - Age (men over 45, women over 55) - Gender (men at higher risk at younger ages) - Family history of heart disease - Race/ethnicity (higher risk for certain populations) ### Modifiable Risk Factors - High blood pressure (hypertension) - High cholesterol - Diabetes - Obesity (BMI > 30) - Smoking - Physical inactivity - Unhealthy diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Chronic stress ## Warning Signs ### Heart Attack Symptoms - Chest pain or discomfort (pressure, squeezing, fullness) - Pain in one or both arms, the back, neck, jaw, or stomach - Shortness of breath - Cold sweat, nausea, lightheadedness - Women may experience atypical symptoms: unusual fatigue, indigestion-like discomfort ### Heart Failure Symptoms - Shortness of breath during activity or when lying down - Fatigue and weakness - Swelling in legs, ankles, and feet - Rapid or irregular heartbeat - Reduced ability to exercise - Persistent cough or wheezing - Swelling of abdomen - Rapid weight gain from fluid retention ## Diagnosis - Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) - Echocardiogram - Stress test - Cardiac catheterization - Blood tests (troponin, BNP, cholesterol) - Coronary angiogram - CT scan or MRI ## Prevention ### Diet - Heart-healthy diet (DASH or Mediterranean) - Limit saturated fat to less than 10% of calories - Limit sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Eat plenty of fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Choose lean proteins - Include omega-3 rich foods ### Exercise - At least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week - Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs", ''relevance_score'': 0.5836054194554425}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': ''regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat mindfully and slowly - Include protein at every meal - Eat plenty of fiber-rich foods - Limit processed and high-calorie foods'', ''relevance_score'': 0.5734395107919992}]','2026-07-28 13:15:07.785241');
