INSERT INTO messages VALUES(20,1,'assistant',unistr('I am an AI Healthcare Assistant, so I can only answer questions related to health, medicine, and wellness. The acronym **FIFA** is not related to the medical field.\u000a\u000aIf you have any health-related questions—such as sports injuries, fitness, or wellness—feel free to ask!'),'[{''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': "# Nutrition and Healthy Eating Guide ## Macronutrients ### Carbohydrates Carbohydrates are the body''s main source of energy. They are found in foods like fruits, vegetables, grains, and legumes. - Recommended intake: 45-65% of total daily calories - Complex carbohydrates (whole grains, vegetables) are preferred over simple sugars - Dietary fiber (25-30 grams per day) is essential for digestive health - Limit added sugars to less than 10% of daily calories ### Proteins Proteins are essential for building and repairing tissues, making enzymes and hormones, and supporting immune function. - Recommended intake: 10-35% of total daily calories - Good sources: lean meats, fish, eggs, dairy, beans, nuts, tofu - Complete proteins contain all essential amino acids - Plant-based proteins (beans, lentils, nuts) are heart-healthy options ### Fats Fats are necessary for nutrient absorption, hormone production, and brain health. - Recommended intake: 20-35% of total daily calories - Unsaturated fats (olive oil, avocados, nuts) are healthier choices - Limit saturated fats to less than 10% of daily calories - Avoid trans fats completely - Omega-3 fatty acids (fish, flaxseed, walnuts) support heart health ## Micronutrients ### Essential Vitamins - Vitamin A: Vision, immune function (carrots, sweet potatoes, spinach) - Vitamin B12: Nerve function, red blood cells (meat, fish, dairy) - Vitamin C: Immune function, skin health (citrus fruits, peppers, berries) - Vitamin D: Bone health, immune function (sunlight, fatty fish, fortified foods) - Vitamin K: Blood clotting, bone health (leafy greens, broccoli) ### Essential Minerals - Calcium: Bone health (dairy, leafy greens, fortified foods) - Iron: Oxygen transport (red meat, spinach, beans) - Potassium: Heart function, blood pressure (bananas, potatoes, avocados) - Zinc: Immune function, wound healing (meat, seeds, legumes) ## Hydration ### Water Intake - General recommendation: 8-10 glasses (2-2.5 liters) per day - Needs increase with exercise, heat, illness, or pregnancy - Signs of dehydration: dark urine, dry mouth, fatigue, headache - Water is the best choice for hydration - Limit sugary drinks and excessive caffeine ### Beverages to Limit - Sugary sodas and energy drinks - Fruit juices with added sugar - Excessive alcohol (1 drink/day women, 2 drinks/day men) - High-caffeine beverages ## Healthy Eating Patterns ### Mediterranean Diet - Rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts - Olive oil as primary fat source - Moderate fish and poultry - Limited red meat - Associated with reduced heart disease, diabetes, and cancer risk ### DASH Diet - Designed to lower blood pressure - High in fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Low in sodium, red meat, added sugars - Rich in potassium, calcium, magnesium ## Dietary Tips for Specific Conditions ### For Diabetes - Monitor carbohydrate intake - Choose low glycemic index foods - Eat regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat", ''relevance_score'': 0.6668550898276318}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''# First Aid Basics ## Emergency Response Principles ### The Chain of Survival 1. Recognize the emergency and call for help 2. Early access to emergency medical services 3. Early CPR with emphasis on chest compressions 4. Early defibrillation (if available) 5. Early advanced care ### When to Call Emergency Services - Difficulty breathing or shortness of breath - Chest pain or pressure - Severe bleeding - Loss of consciousness - Suspected stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, speech difficulty) - Severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis) - Seizures - Severe burns - Head, neck, or spinal injuries - Drowning - Poisoning ## CPR (Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation) ### Hands-Only CPR for Adults 1. Check if the person is responsive 2. Call 911 or ask someone to call 3. Push hard and fast in the center of the chest 4. Rate: 100-120 compressions per minute 5. Depth: at least 2 inches (5 cm) 6. Allow full chest recoil between compressions 7. Continue until emergency services arrive ### CPR with Rescue Breaths After 30 chest compressions, give 2 rescue breaths: 1. Tilt the head back, lift the chin 2. Pinch the nose shut 3. Create a complete seal over the mouth 4. Give 2 breaths, each lasting 1 second 5. Continue 30:2 ratio ## Choking Response ### Conscious Adult/Child 1. Ask "Are you choking?" 2. If the person cannot speak, cough, or breathe: - Stand behind the person - Make a fist with one hand - Place the fist above the navel - Grasp the fist with the other hand - Perform quick upward thrusts 3. Continue until the object is expelled or the person becomes unconscious ### Unconscious Adult 1. Call 911 2. Begin CPR 3. Look for the object in the mouth before giving breaths 4. If visible, remove it 5. Continue CPR ## Bleeding Control ### Direct Pressure 1. Apply firm, direct pressure with a clean cloth or gauze 2. Maintain pressure for at least 15 minutes 3. Add more layers if blood soaks through 4. Do not remove the first layer of cloth ### Severe Bleeding 1. Call 911 immediately 2. Apply firm pressure 3. If bleeding is from an extremity, consider a tourniquet 4. Keep the person warm and calm 5. Do not remove the object causing the wound ## Burns ### First-Degree Burns - Affects only the outer layer of skin - Treatment: Cool water for 10 minutes, aloe vera, OTC pain relievers ### Second-Degree Burns - Affects outer and underlying layers - Treatment: Cool water, do not pop blisters, seek medical attention ### Third-Degree Burns - Affects all layers of skin - Treatment: Call 911, do not remove clothing, cover with clean cloth, do not apply water ## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6636468491605071}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6628374906289016}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "# Heart Disease - Overview ## What is Heart Disease? Heart disease, also known as cardiovascular disease, refers to conditions that involve narrowed or blocked blood vessels that can lead to a heart attack, chest pain (angina), or stroke. It is the leading cause of death worldwide. Common heart diseases include coronary artery disease, heart failure, arrhythmias, and valve problems. ## Types of Heart Disease ### Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) The most common type of heart disease. CAD occurs when the coronary arteries become narrowed or blocked due to plaque buildup (atherosclerosis). This reduces blood flow to the heart muscle and can lead to heart attack. ### Heart Failure Heart failure means the heart cannot pump blood as well as it should. Despite the name, it does not mean the heart has stopped working. It means the heart is not pumping as well as the body needs. ### Arrhythmias Arrhythmias are irregular heartbeats. The heart may beat too quickly (tachycardia), too slowly (bradycardia), or with an irregular pattern. ### Heart Valve Disease When one or more heart valves do not work properly, the heart must work harder to pump blood through the body. ## Risk Factors ### Non-Modifiable Risk Factors - Age (men over 45, women over 55) - Gender (men at higher risk at younger ages) - Family history of heart disease - Race/ethnicity (higher risk for certain populations) ### Modifiable Risk Factors - High blood pressure (hypertension) - High cholesterol - Diabetes - Obesity (BMI > 30) - Smoking - Physical inactivity - Unhealthy diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Chronic stress ## Warning Signs ### Heart Attack Symptoms - Chest pain or discomfort (pressure, squeezing, fullness) - Pain in one or both arms, the back, neck, jaw, or stomach - Shortness of breath - Cold sweat, nausea, lightheadedness - Women may experience atypical symptoms: unusual fatigue, indigestion-like discomfort ### Heart Failure Symptoms - Shortness of breath during activity or when lying down - Fatigue and weakness - Swelling in legs, ankles, and feet - Rapid or irregular heartbeat - Reduced ability to exercise - Persistent cough or wheezing - Swelling of abdomen - Rapid weight gain from fluid retention ## Diagnosis - Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) - Echocardiogram - Stress test - Cardiac catheterization - Blood tests (troponin, BNP, cholesterol) - Coronary angiogram - CT scan or MRI ## Prevention ### Diet - Heart-healthy diet (DASH or Mediterranean) - Limit saturated fat to less than 10% of calories - Limit sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Eat plenty of fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Choose lean proteins - Include omega-3 rich foods ### Exercise - At least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week - Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs", ''relevance_score'': 0.6617321926454934}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6596671921230716}]','2026-07-28 13:17:12.100303');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(21,6,'user','getting eye infection like reddish what to do','','2026-07-28 13:25:39.055821');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(22,6,'assistant',unistr('If you''re experiencing a **reddish eye infection**, here are the steps you can take for relief and when to seek medical attention:\u000a\u000a### **Common Symptoms of Eye Infections**\u000a- Redness in the white of the eye or inner eyelid\u000a- Increased tear production\u000a- Thick yellow/green discharge (possible bacterial infection)\u000a- Watery discharge (possible viral infection)\u000a- Itching, burning, or irritation\u000a- Sensitivity to light\u000a- Swollen eyelids\u000a- Blurred vision\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Immediate Home Care (For Mild Cases)**\u000a1. **Wash Hands Frequently**\u000a   - Avoid touching or rubbing your eyes to prevent spreading infection.\u000a\u000a2. **Warm or Cold Compress**\u000a   - **Warm compress** (for bacterial infections with discharge) – Helps loosen crust and soothe irritation.\u000a   - **Cold compress** (for viral/allergic conjunctivitis) – Reduces swelling and itching.\u000a\u000a3. **Artificial Tears (Lubricating Eye Drops)**\u000a   - Helps flush out irritants and relieve dryness (avoid redness-relief drops like Visine, as they can worsen irritation).\u000a\u000a4. **Avoid Contact Lenses & Eye Makeup**\u000a   - Stop using contacts until the infection clears.\u000a   - Discard old eye makeup to prevent reinfection.\u000a\u000a5. **Clean Discharge Gently**\u000a   - Use a clean, damp cloth to wipe away discharge from the eyes.\u000a\u000a6. **Avoid Sharing Personal Items**\u000a   - Towels, pillows, or eye drops should not be shared to prevent spreading infection.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **When to See a Doctor**\u000aSeek **immediate medical attention** if you experience:\u000a✔ **Severe pain** in the eye\u000a✔ **Blurred or decreased vision**\u000a✔ **Extreme light sensitivity**\u000a✔ **Thick, pus-like discharge** (possible bacterial infection)\u000a✔ **High fever** or signs of systemic illness\u000a✔ **No improvement after 2–3 days** of home care\u000a✔ **History of eye conditions** (e.g., glaucoma, recent eye surgery)\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Possible Causes & Treatments**\u000a| **Type of Infection** | **Common Causes** | **Treatment** |\u000a|----------------------|------------------|--------------|\u000a| **Bacterial Conjunctivitis** | Bacteria (e.g., *Staphylococcus*) | Antibiotic eye drops/ointment (prescribed by a doctor) |\u000a| **Viral Conjunctivitis** | Viruses (e.g., adenovirus) | Self-limiting (lasts 1–2 weeks); artificial tears, cold compress |\u000a| **Allergic Conjunctivitis** | Allergens (pollen, dust, pet dander) | Antihistamine eye drops, avoiding triggers |\u000a| **Stye (Hordeolum)** | Bacterial infection of eyelash follicle | Warm compress, antibiotic ointment (if severe) |\u000a| **Blepharitis** | Inflammation of eyelid margins | Eyelid hygiene, warm compress, antibiotics (if bacterial) |\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Prevention Tips**\u000a- Wash hands frequently, especially before touching your face.\u000a- Avoid sharing towels, pillows, or eye makeup.\u000a- Replace eye makeup every 3–6 months.\u000a- Wear protective eyewear in dusty or windy environments.\u000a- Follow proper contact lens hygiene (if applicable).\u000a\u000a**I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional for eye infections, especially if symptoms worsen or persist.**'),'[{''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7034767862368351}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6950391997978456}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6726525829518767}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': "# Fever, Cold, and Influenza (Flu) ## Fever ### What is Fever? Fever is an increase in the body''s temperature, which is regulated by the hypothalamus. A normal body temperature is approximately 98.6°F (37°C). A fever is generally considered to be a temperature of 100.4°F (38°C) or higher. ### Causes of Fever - Infections (bacterial, viral, fungal) - Inflammatory conditions - Medications - Heat stroke - Certain cancers - Autoimmune disorders ### When to See a Doctor - Infants under 3 months: any fever over 100.4°F (38°C) - Children 3 months to 3 years: fever of 102.2°F (39°C) or higher - Adults: fever above 103°F (39.4°C) or fever lasting more than 3 days - Anyone with fever accompanied by severe headache, stiff neck, confusion, or rash ### Fever Management - Stay hydrated by drinking plenty of fluids - Rest adequately - Dress in lightweight clothing - Take acetaminophen or ibuprofen (as directed) - Lukewarm sponge bath if temperature is very high - Monitor temperature regularly ## Common Cold ### What is a Cold? The common cold is a viral infection of your nose and throat (upper respiratory tract). It is usually harmless, although it might not feel that way. Many types of viruses can cause a cold, but rhinoviruses are the most common. ### Symptoms - Runny or stuffy nose - Sore throat - Cough - Congestion - Slight body aches - Sneezing - Low-grade fever - Generally feeling unwell (malaise) ### Duration - Onset: 1-3 days after exposure - Duration: 7-10 days - Cough may persist for 2 weeks ### Treatment - Rest - Stay hydrated - Saline nasal drops or spray - Warm liquids (tea, broth) - Honey (for adults and children over 1 year) - Over-the-counter cold medications (for adults) - Gargle with warm salt water for sore throat ## Influenza (Flu) ### What is the Flu? Influenza (flu) is a contagious respiratory illness caused by influenza viruses. It can cause mild to severe illness, and at times can lead to death. Flu is different from a cold. ### Flu vs Cold The flu and the common cold have similar symptoms, but the flu is generally more severe: - Flu: sudden onset, high fever (100-104°F), severe body aches, extreme fatigue - Cold: gradual onset, low or no fever, mild aches, mild fatigue ### Flu Symptoms - Fever (usually high) - Severe body aches - Headache - Fatigue (can last 2-3 weeks) - Dry cough - Sore throat - Nasal congestion - Chills - Sweating ### When to Seek Emergency Care - Difficulty breathing or shortness of breath - Persistent pain or pressure in chest - Sudden dizziness or confusion - Severe or persistent vomiting - Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours -", ''relevance_score'': 0.6654275246170235}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.6637488721179057}]','2026-07-28 13:25:52.199074');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(23,6,'user','please pefer some drops','','2026-07-28 13:26:35.208858');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(24,6,'assistant',unistr('For **red, irritated, or infected eyes**, the appropriate **eye drops** depend on the **cause** of the problem. Below are **common over-the-counter (OTC) and prescription eye drops** that may help, but **consult an eye doctor (ophthalmologist/optometrist) before use**, especially if symptoms persist or worsen.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **1. For Dryness & Mild Irritation (Artificial Tears)**\u000a**Best for:** Redness due to dryness, wind, smoke, or screen use.\u000a**How they work:** Lubricate the eye and flush out irritants.\u000a**Examples (OTC):**\u000a- **Preservative-free options (best for frequent use):**\u000a  - Systane Ultra\u000a  - Refresh Tears\u000a  - TheraTears\u000a  - Blink Tears\u000a- **With preservatives (for occasional use):**\u000a  - Visine Tears\u000a  - GenTeal Tears\u000a\u000a⚠️ **Avoid "redness-relief" drops** (e.g., Visine Original, Clear Eyes) for long-term use—they can worsen redness over time.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **2. For Allergic Conjunctivitis (Itchy, Watery Eyes)**\u000a**Best for:** Redness, itching, and swelling due to allergies (pollen, dust, pet dander).\u000a**How they work:** Block histamine (allergic reaction).\u000a**Examples (OTC):**\u000a- **Antihistamine drops:**\u000a  - Ketotifen (Zaditor, Alaway) – Fast relief, lasts 12 hours\u000a  - Olopatadine (Pataday, Patanol) – Prescription-strength (some OTC versions available)\u000a- **Mast cell stabilizers (preventative):**\u000a  - Cromolyn sodium (Crolom) – Takes a few days to work\u000a\u000a**Oral antihistamines** (e.g., cetirizine/Zyrtec, loratadine/Claritin) can also help.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **3. For Bacterial Conjunctivitis (Pink Eye with Discharge)**\u000a**Best for:** Thick yellow/green discharge, crusty eyelids, redness.\u000a**How they work:** Kill bacteria causing infection.\u000a⚠️ **Requires a prescription** (do not use OTC antibiotic drops without a doctor’s advice).\u000a**Examples (Prescription Only):**\u000a- **Broad-spectrum antibiotics:**\u000a  - Ciprofloxacin (Ciloxan)\u000a  - Ofloxacin (Ocuflox)\u000a  - Tobramycin (Tobrex)\u000a  - Erythromycin ointment (for severe cases)\u000a- **Combination drops (antibiotic + steroid):**\u000a  - Tobramycin/dexamethasone (Tobradex) – For inflammation + infection\u000a\u000a❌ **Do NOT use steroid drops (e.g., prednisolone) without a doctor’s supervision**—they can worsen viral/fungal infections.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **4. For Viral Conjunctivitis (Watery, Red Eyes)**\u000a**Best for:** Redness, watery discharge, often with a cold or flu.\u000a**How they work:** Viral infections usually **resolve on their own** in 1–2 weeks.\u000a**Supportive care (OTC):**\u000a- Artificial tears (e.g., Systane, Refresh)\u000a- Cold compresses\u000a- Lubricating ointments (e.g., Refresh PM) at night\u000a\u000a⚠️ **Antibiotics do NOT work for viral infections.**\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **5. For Contact Lens-Related Irritation**\u000a**Best for:** Redness, discomfort, or dryness from contacts.\u000a**How they work:** Rewetting drops designed for contact lens wearers.\u000a**Examples (OTC):**\u000a- Blink Contacts\u000a- Opti-Free Puremoist Rewetting Drops\u000a- Refresh Contacts\u000a\u000a❌ **Do NOT wear contacts** if your eye is red, painful, or has discharge—switch to glasses until healed.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **6. For Eye Strain & Computer Use (Digital Eye Strain)**\u000a**Best for:** Redness, dryness, or fatigue from prolonged screen time.\u000a**How they work:** Lubricate and reduce strain.\u000a**Examples (OTC):**\u000a- Systane Complete\u000a- Rohto Cool Max (cooling effect)\u000a- TheraTears Computer Eye Drops\u000a\u000a**Other tips:**\u000a- Follow the **20-20-20 rule** (every 20 mins, look 20 feet away for 20 sec).\u000a- Adjust screen brightness and use blue light filters.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **When to See a Doctor Immediately**\u000a🚨 **Seek emergency care if you have:**\u000a- Severe pain in the eye\u000a- Sudden vision changes (blurriness, double vision)\u000a- Extreme light sensitivity\u000a-'),'[{''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6594199221325618}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.645158400600936}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6437111712524558}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': ''regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat mindfully and slowly - Include protein at every meal - Eat plenty of fiber-rich foods - Limit processed and high-calorie foods'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6377970637460355}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.6324790543937343}]','2026-07-28 13:26:55.686127');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(25,6,'user','any home made remedy','','2026-07-28 13:27:19.746906');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(26,6,'assistant',unistr('Here are some **safe and effective homemade remedies** for **mild eye redness, irritation, or infections** (like conjunctivitis). However, **consult a doctor if symptoms worsen or persist beyond 2–3 days**, as some conditions (e.g., bacterial infections) require medical treatment.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **✅ 1. Warm Compress (Best for Styes, Bacterial Conjunctivitis, or Crusty Eyes)**\u000a**How it helps:**\u000a- Loosens dried discharge (common in bacterial infections).\u000a- Improves blood circulation and soothes irritation.\u000a- Helps drain a **stye** (infected eyelash follicle).\u000a\u000a**How to make:**\u000a1. Soak a **clean washcloth** in **warm (not hot) water**.\u000a2. Wring out excess water.\u000a3. Place over **closed eyelids** for **5–10 minutes**, 3–4 times daily.\u000a4. Gently wipe away discharge with a **fresh, damp cloth**.\u000a\u000a⚠️ **Do NOT use boiling water**—it can burn the delicate eye area.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **✅ 2. Cold Compress (Best for Allergies, Viral Conjunctivitis, or Swelling)**\u000a**How it helps:**\u000a- Reduces **itching, swelling, and redness** (especially for allergies).\u000a- Soothes **viral conjunctivitis** (often watery, itchy eyes).\u000a\u000a**How to make:**\u000a1. Wrap **ice cubes** in a **clean cloth** (or use a chilled spoon).\u000a2. Hold over **closed eyes** for **5–10 minutes**, 2–3 times daily.\u000a3. Alternatively, soak a cloth in **cold water** and apply.\u000a\u000a❌ **Do NOT apply ice directly** to the skin—it can cause frostbite.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **✅ 3. Saline Solution (For Flushing Irritants & Mild Infections)**\u000a**How it helps:**\u000a- Cleanses the eye of **dust, pollen, or discharge**.\u000a- Helps with **mild bacterial/viral conjunctivitis**.\u000a\u000a**How to make:**\u000a1. **Boil 1 cup of distilled or filtered water** (to sterilize).\u000a2. Add **½ tsp salt** (non-iodized, like sea salt or kosher salt).\u000a3. Let it cool to **room temperature**.\u000a4. Use a **clean dropper or eyecup** to rinse the eye.\u000a5. **Blink gently** to spread the solution.\u000a\u000a⚠️ **Do NOT use tap water** (may contain bacteria/chemicals).\u000a❌ **Avoid if you have a severe infection** (see a doctor instead).\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **✅ 4. Green Tea Bags (Anti-Inflammatory & Soothing)**\u000a**How it helps:**\u000a- **Catechins** in green tea reduce inflammation.\u000a- Helps with **puffiness, redness, and mild infections**.\u000a\u000a**How to use:**\u000a1. Steep **2 green tea bags** in **hot water** for 2–3 minutes.\u000a2. Remove and let them **cool in the fridge** (10–15 mins).\u000a3. Place over **closed eyes** for **10–15 minutes**.\u000a4. Repeat **2–3 times daily**.\u000a\u000a✅ **Best for:** Allergies, tired eyes, or mild irritation.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **✅ 5. Honey Eye Drops (For Bacterial Infections – Use with Caution!)**\u000a**How it helps:**\u000a- **Raw honey** has **antibacterial properties** (studies show it may help with *Staphylococcus* infections).\u000a- **Manuka honey** is the most effective.\u000a\u000a**How to make (diluted version):**\u000a1. Mix **1 part raw honey** with **2 parts sterile saline solution** (or boiled & cooled water).\u000a2. Stir well until fully dissolved.\u000a3. Use a **clean dropper** to apply **1–2 drops** in the affected eye, **2–3 times daily**.\u000a\u000a⚠️ **Warnings:**\u000a- **Do NOT use undiluted honey** (can sting and worsen irritation).\u000a- **Not for viral infections** (honey won’t help).\u000a- **Stop if irritation increases** (some people are allergic).\u000a- **Best for mild bacterial cases**—see a doctor if no improvement in **24–48 hours**.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **✅ 6. Aloe Vera Gel (For Soothing Irritation & Redness)**\u000a**How it helps:**\u000a- **Anti-inflammatory & cooling** effect.\u000a- Helps with **dryness, itching, and mild infections**.\u000a\u000a**How to use:**\u000a1. Extract **fresh aloe vera gel** (from a plant or **1'),'[{''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.716087031900902}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7122745579846339}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.7015262608794925}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': ''regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat mindfully and slowly - Include protein at every meal - Eat plenty of fiber-rich foods - Limit processed and high-calorie foods'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6846247900994525}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.6845143247644524}]','2026-07-28 13:27:36.543420');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(27,6,'user','whether to drink milk or not','','2026-07-28 13:29:39.186810');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(28,6,'assistant',unistr('Whether to **drink milk** depends on your **health goals, digestive tolerance, and medical conditions**. Here’s a **science-backed breakdown** to help you decide:\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **✅ Benefits of Drinking Milk**\u000a#### **1. Rich in Essential Nutrients**\u000a- **Calcium** (bone health, muscle function, nerve signaling)\u000a- **Vitamin D** (if fortified – supports immunity & calcium absorption)\u000a- **Protein** (~8g per cup – helps muscle repair & satiety)\u000a- **Vitamin B12** (supports red blood cells & brain function)\u000a- **Phosphorus & Potassium** (heart & kidney health)\u000a\u000a#### **2. Bone Health (Especially for Children & Older Adults)**\u000a- **Reduces risk of osteoporosis** (when combined with vitamin D & weight-bearing exercise).\u000a- **Critical for growing children** (supports bone development).\u000a\u000a#### **3. Muscle Growth & Recovery**\u000a- **Whey & casein proteins** in milk help **repair muscles** after exercise.\u000a- **Better than plant-based milks** for muscle synthesis (unless fortified).\u000a\u000a#### **4. Hydration & Electrolytes**\u000a- Contains **sodium & potassium**, which help **replenish fluids** (useful after sweating).\u000a\u000a#### **5. May Support Heart Health (If Low-Fat)**\u000a- Some studies link **low-fat dairy** to **lower blood pressure** (due to bioactive peptides).\u000a- **Full-fat dairy** may increase LDL ("bad" cholesterol) in some people.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **❌ Potential Downsides of Drinking Milk**\u000a#### **1. Lactose Intolerance (Digestive Issues)**\u000a- **Symptoms:** Bloating, gas, diarrhea, stomach cramps.\u000a- **Who’s affected?** ~65% of adults worldwide (varies by ethnicity).\u000a- **Solution:**\u000a  - Try **lactose-free milk** (e.g., Lactaid).\u000a  - **Fermented dairy** (yogurt, kefir) is often better tolerated.\u000a  - **Small amounts** (e.g., ½ cup) may be okay.\u000a\u000a#### **2. Milk Allergy (Immune Reaction)**\u000a- **Symptoms:** Hives, swelling, wheezing, vomiting, anaphylaxis (rare but serious).\u000a- **Who’s affected?** ~2–3% of children (most outgrow it by age 5).\u000a- **Solution:** **Avoid all dairy** (use fortified plant-based milks).\u000a\u000a#### **3. Acne & Skin Issues**\u000a- Some studies link **skim milk** to **increased acne** (possibly due to hormones).\u000a- **Full-fat dairy** may have a **neutral or protective effect** on skin.\u000a\u000a#### **4. Possible Link to Certain Cancers (Debated)**\u000a- **Prostate cancer:** Some studies suggest **high dairy intake may increase risk** (due to IGF-1 hormone).\u000a- **Breast/ovarian cancer:** **No strong evidence**, but some research suggests a **possible link** (needs more study).\u000a- **Colorectal cancer:** **May have a protective effect** (due to calcium).\u000a\u000a#### **5. Hormones & Antibiotics (Conventional Milk Concerns)**\u000a- **rBGH (growth hormone):** Banned in **EU & Canada**, but **legal in the US** (may increase IGF-1).\u000a- **Antibiotics:** Rarely present in **pasteurized milk** (FDA regulates this).\u000a- **Solution:** Choose **organic, grass-fed, or hormone-free milk** if concerned.\u000a\u000a#### **6. Weight Gain (If Consumed in Excess)**\u000a- **Full-fat milk** is **calorie-dense** (~150 kcal/cup).\u000a- **Flavored milk** (chocolate, strawberry) has **added sugar**.\u000a- **Solution:** Stick to **plain, unsweetened milk** and **moderate portions**.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **🥛 Best Types of Milk (Ranked by Health Benefits)**\u000a| **Type** | **Pros** | **Cons** | **Best For** |\u000a|----------|---------|---------|-------------|\u000a| **Whole Milk (3.25% fat)** | Rich in healthy fats, vitamin K2, satiety | Higher in calories & saturated fat | Kids, muscle gain, those who tolerate fat well |\u000a| **Low-Fat (1-2% fat)** | Lower in calories, still has protein & calcium | Less vitamin K2, may spike insulin more | Weight management, heart health (if no lactose issues) |\u000a| **Skim Milk (0% fat)** | Lowest calorie, high protein | Less satiety, may lack fat-soluble vitamins | Weight loss, lactose-intolerant people (if fortified) |\u000a| **Organic Milk** | No synthetic hormones/pesticides, higher omega'),'[{''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': "# Nutrition and Healthy Eating Guide ## Macronutrients ### Carbohydrates Carbohydrates are the body''s main source of energy. They are found in foods like fruits, vegetables, grains, and legumes. - Recommended intake: 45-65% of total daily calories - Complex carbohydrates (whole grains, vegetables) are preferred over simple sugars - Dietary fiber (25-30 grams per day) is essential for digestive health - Limit added sugars to less than 10% of daily calories ### Proteins Proteins are essential for building and repairing tissues, making enzymes and hormones, and supporting immune function. - Recommended intake: 10-35% of total daily calories - Good sources: lean meats, fish, eggs, dairy, beans, nuts, tofu - Complete proteins contain all essential amino acids - Plant-based proteins (beans, lentils, nuts) are heart-healthy options ### Fats Fats are necessary for nutrient absorption, hormone production, and brain health. - Recommended intake: 20-35% of total daily calories - Unsaturated fats (olive oil, avocados, nuts) are healthier choices - Limit saturated fats to less than 10% of daily calories - Avoid trans fats completely - Omega-3 fatty acids (fish, flaxseed, walnuts) support heart health ## Micronutrients ### Essential Vitamins - Vitamin A: Vision, immune function (carrots, sweet potatoes, spinach) - Vitamin B12: Nerve function, red blood cells (meat, fish, dairy) - Vitamin C: Immune function, skin health (citrus fruits, peppers, berries) - Vitamin D: Bone health, immune function (sunlight, fatty fish, fortified foods) - Vitamin K: Blood clotting, bone health (leafy greens, broccoli) ### Essential Minerals - Calcium: Bone health (dairy, leafy greens, fortified foods) - Iron: Oxygen transport (red meat, spinach, beans) - Potassium: Heart function, blood pressure (bananas, potatoes, avocados) - Zinc: Immune function, wound healing (meat, seeds, legumes) ## Hydration ### Water Intake - General recommendation: 8-10 glasses (2-2.5 liters) per day - Needs increase with exercise, heat, illness, or pregnancy - Signs of dehydration: dark urine, dry mouth, fatigue, headache - Water is the best choice for hydration - Limit sugary drinks and excessive caffeine ### Beverages to Limit - Sugary sodas and energy drinks - Fruit juices with added sugar - Excessive alcohol (1 drink/day women, 2 drinks/day men) - High-caffeine beverages ## Healthy Eating Patterns ### Mediterranean Diet - Rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts - Olive oil as primary fat source - Moderate fish and poultry - Limited red meat - Associated with reduced heart disease, diabetes, and cancer risk ### DASH Diet - Designed to lower blood pressure - High in fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Low in sodium, red meat, added sugars - Rich in potassium, calcium, magnesium ## Dietary Tips for Specific Conditions ### For Diabetes - Monitor carbohydrate intake - Choose low glycemic index foods - Eat regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat", ''relevance_score'': 0.6475202434566438}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': ''regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat mindfully and slowly - Include protein at every meal - Eat plenty of fiber-rich foods - Limit processed and high-calorie foods'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6452043554714431}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6394119863068244}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6367702358523402}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6215816433034899}]','2026-07-28 13:29:56.312769');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(29,6,'user','my doctor said don''t take milk','','2026-07-28 13:31:30.920964');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(30,6,'assistant',unistr('If your **doctor advised you to avoid milk**, it’s likely due to one of the following medical reasons. Here’s what you should know and **what to do instead**:\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **🚫 Why Your Doctor May Have Told You to Avoid Milk**\u000a#### **1. Lactose Intolerance (Most Common Reason)**\u000a- **What happens?** Your body **lacks lactase**, the enzyme needed to digest **lactose (milk sugar)**.\u000a- **Symptoms:**\u000a  - Bloating, gas, diarrhea, stomach cramps (30 min–2 hrs after drinking milk).\u000a- **Solution:**\u000a  - **Lactose-free milk** (e.g., Lactaid) – contains lactase enzyme.\u000a  - **Fermented dairy** (yogurt, kefir) – bacteria help digest lactose.\u000a  - **Small amounts** (e.g., ½ cup) may be tolerated.\u000a  - **Plant-based milks** (almond, soy, oat) – check for **calcium & vitamin D fortification**.\u000a\u000a#### **2. Milk Allergy (Immune Reaction to Milk Proteins)**\u000a- **What happens?** Your immune system **overreacts to casein or whey** (milk proteins).\u000a- **Symptoms:**\u000a  - **Mild:** Hives, itching, swelling, vomiting.\u000a  - **Severe (anaphylaxis):** Difficulty breathing, throat swelling, drop in blood pressure (requires **epinephrine/EpiPen**).\u000a- **Solution:**\u000a  - **Avoid ALL dairy products** (milk, cheese, yogurt, butter, cream).\u000a  - **Read labels carefully** – milk proteins hide in many foods (e.g., baked goods, sauces, processed meats).\u000a  - **Use plant-based alternatives** (soy, almond, oat, coconut milk).\u000a\u000a#### **3. Digestive Disorders (IBS, GERD, Gastritis)**\u000a- **Why?** Milk can **worsen symptoms** in conditions like:\u000a  - **IBS (Irritable Bowel Syndrome)** – Lactose can trigger diarrhea/constipation.\u000a  - **GERD (Acid Reflux)** – Whole milk may relax the **lower esophageal sphincter**, causing heartburn.\u000a  - **Gastritis** – Dairy can irritate the stomach lining.\u000a- **Solution:**\u000a  - **Low-fat or lactose-free milk** (if tolerated).\u000a  - **Avoid milk if it triggers symptoms** (keep a food diary).\u000a  - **Try non-dairy alternatives** (e.g., almond milk for GERD, oat milk for IBS).\u000a\u000a#### **4. Acne or Skin Issues**\u000a- **Why?** Some studies link **skim milk** to **increased acne** (possibly due to hormones like IGF-1).\u000a- **Solution:**\u000a  - **Switch to full-fat dairy** (may have a **neutral or protective effect** on skin).\u000a  - **Avoid milk if it worsens acne** (try eliminating for 4–6 weeks to test).\u000a  - **Non-dairy alternatives** (e.g., almond milk, coconut yogurt).\u000a\u000a#### **5. Kidney Stones or High Calcium Levels**\u000a- **Why?** Excess calcium (from milk + supplements) can **increase kidney stone risk** in susceptible people.\u000a- **Solution:**\u000a  - **Moderate milk intake** (1–2 servings/day).\u000a  - **Drink plenty of water** (helps flush out calcium).\u000a  - **Avoid calcium supplements** unless prescribed.\u000a\u000a#### **6. Autoimmune or Inflammatory Conditions**\u000a- **Why?** Some people with **autoimmune diseases** (e.g., Hashimoto’s, rheumatoid arthritis) report **less inflammation** when avoiding dairy.\u000a- **Solution:**\u000a  - **Try an elimination diet** (remove dairy for 3–4 weeks, then reintroduce to check for reactions).\u000a  - **Non-dairy alternatives** (e.g., coconut milk, cashew cheese).\u000a\u000a#### **7. Migraines or Headaches**\u000a- **Why?** Milk contains **tyramine**, which may trigger migraines in some people.\u000a- **Solution:**\u000a  - **Avoid aged cheeses & fermented dairy** (higher in tyramine).\u000a  - **Try lactose-free milk** (lower in tyramine).\u000a  - **Keep a headache diary** to track triggers.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **✅ What to Drink Instead of Milk? (Nutrient-Rich Alternatives)**\u000a| **Alternative** | **Pros** | **Cons** | **Best For** |\u000a|----------------|---------|---------|-------------|\u000a| **Soy Milk** | High in **protein (7–9g/cup)**, fortified with **calcium & vitamin D** | Some brands have **added sugar** | Muscle building,'),'[{''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6677778466445273}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6582850033169644}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': ''regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat mindfully and slowly - Include protein at every meal - Eat plenty of fiber-rich foods - Limit processed and high-calorie foods'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6552618113039534}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.655079181355314}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.6489186388024372}]','2026-07-28 13:32:51.117630');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(31,6,'user','my doctor suggested to don''t drink milk','','2026-07-28 13:32:55.532714');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(32,6,'assistant',unistr('Since your **doctor specifically advised against drinking milk**, it’s important to **follow their guidance**—they likely identified a **medical reason** (e.g., lactose intolerance, allergy, digestive issues, or another condition). Below is a **detailed action plan** to help you **replace milk safely** while ensuring you still get essential nutrients.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **🔍 Step 1: Confirm Why Your Doctor Said "No Milk"**\u000aAsk your doctor for clarification if you’re unsure. Common reasons include:\u000a✅ **Lactose intolerance** (digestive issues like bloating, gas, diarrhea)\u000a✅ **Milk allergy** (immune reaction—hives, swelling, breathing problems)\u000a✅ **Digestive disorders** (IBS, GERD, gastritis, Crohn’s disease)\u000a✅ **Acne or skin inflammation** (milk may worsen breakouts)\u000a✅ **Kidney stones or high calcium levels** (excess calcium can be harmful)\u000a✅ **Autoimmune conditions** (e.g., Hashimoto’s, rheumatoid arthritis—dairy may trigger inflammation)\u000a✅ **Migraines or headaches** (tyramine in milk may be a trigger)\u000a\u000a**If you don’t know the reason, ask your doctor:**\u000a*"Can you clarify why I should avoid milk? Is it an allergy, intolerance, or another concern?"*\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **🥛 Step 2: Find the Best Milk Alternatives (Based on Your Needs)**\u000a#### **🔹 If You Have Lactose Intolerance (Digestive Issues)**\u000a✔ **Lactose-free milk** (e.g., Lactaid) – Tastes like regular milk, same nutrients.\u000a✔ **Fermented dairy** (yogurt, kefir) – Easier to digest (bacteria break down lactose).\u000a✔ **Plant-based milks** (see table below).\u000a\u000a#### **🔹 If You Have a Milk Allergy (Immune Reaction)**\u000a❌ **Avoid ALL dairy products** (milk, cheese, yogurt, butter, cream, whey, casein).\u000a✔ **Use fortified plant-based milks** (soy, almond, oat, coconut, pea milk).\u000a⚠️ **Check labels**—some processed foods contain hidden dairy (e.g., bread, sauces, protein bars).\u000a\u000a#### **🔹 If You Have IBS, GERD, or Gastritis**\u000a✔ **Low-FODMAP plant milks** (almond, rice, or oat milk—**avoid soy if it triggers symptoms**).\u000a✔ **Lactose-free milk** (if tolerated).\u000a❌ **Avoid high-fat dairy** (whole milk, cream—can worsen reflux).\u000a\u000a#### **🔹 If You Have Acne or Skin Issues**\u000a✔ **Unsweetened almond or coconut milk** (lower in hormones than dairy).\u000a✔ **Avoid skim milk** (linked to acne in some studies).\u000a❌ **Avoid flavored milks** (added sugar can worsen skin).\u000a\u000a#### **🔹 If You Have Kidney Stones or High Calcium**\u000a✔ **Calcium-fortified plant milks** (but **limit to 1–2 servings/day**).\u000a✔ **Drink plenty of water** (helps flush out excess calcium).\u000a❌ **Avoid calcium supplements** unless prescribed.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **📊 Best Milk Alternatives (Nutrient Comparison)**\u000a| **Milk Type** | **Calories (per cup)** | **Protein (g)** | **Calcium (% DV)** | **Vitamin D (% DV)** | **Best For** | **Avoid If** |\u000a|--------------|----------------------|----------------|-------------------|---------------------|-------------|-------------|\u000a| **Cow’s Milk (Whole)** | 150 | 8 | 30% | 15% | General nutrition | Allergy, lactose intolerance |\u000a| **Lactose-Free Milk** | 150 | 8 | 30% | 15% | Lactose intolerance | Milk allergy |\u000a| **Soy Milk** | 80–100 | 7–9 | 30% | 15% | Protein, muscle building | Soy allergy, IBS (if sensitive) |\u000a| **Almond Milk** | 30–60 | 1 | 45% | 15% | Low-calorie, weight loss | Nut allergy, low protein needs |\u000a| **Oat Milk** | 120–160 | 2–4 | 25% | 15% |'),'[{''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6896913010505161}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': ''regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat mindfully and slowly - Include protein at every meal - Eat plenty of fiber-rich foods - Limit processed and high-calorie foods'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6835010927981895}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': "# Nutrition and Healthy Eating Guide ## Macronutrients ### Carbohydrates Carbohydrates are the body''s main source of energy. They are found in foods like fruits, vegetables, grains, and legumes. - Recommended intake: 45-65% of total daily calories - Complex carbohydrates (whole grains, vegetables) are preferred over simple sugars - Dietary fiber (25-30 grams per day) is essential for digestive health - Limit added sugars to less than 10% of daily calories ### Proteins Proteins are essential for building and repairing tissues, making enzymes and hormones, and supporting immune function. - Recommended intake: 10-35% of total daily calories - Good sources: lean meats, fish, eggs, dairy, beans, nuts, tofu - Complete proteins contain all essential amino acids - Plant-based proteins (beans, lentils, nuts) are heart-healthy options ### Fats Fats are necessary for nutrient absorption, hormone production, and brain health. - Recommended intake: 20-35% of total daily calories - Unsaturated fats (olive oil, avocados, nuts) are healthier choices - Limit saturated fats to less than 10% of daily calories - Avoid trans fats completely - Omega-3 fatty acids (fish, flaxseed, walnuts) support heart health ## Micronutrients ### Essential Vitamins - Vitamin A: Vision, immune function (carrots, sweet potatoes, spinach) - Vitamin B12: Nerve function, red blood cells (meat, fish, dairy) - Vitamin C: Immune function, skin health (citrus fruits, peppers, berries) - Vitamin D: Bone health, immune function (sunlight, fatty fish, fortified foods) - Vitamin K: Blood clotting, bone health (leafy greens, broccoli) ### Essential Minerals - Calcium: Bone health (dairy, leafy greens, fortified foods) - Iron: Oxygen transport (red meat, spinach, beans) - Potassium: Heart function, blood pressure (bananas, potatoes, avocados) - Zinc: Immune function, wound healing (meat, seeds, legumes) ## Hydration ### Water Intake - General recommendation: 8-10 glasses (2-2.5 liters) per day - Needs increase with exercise, heat, illness, or pregnancy - Signs of dehydration: dark urine, dry mouth, fatigue, headache - Water is the best choice for hydration - Limit sugary drinks and excessive caffeine ### Beverages to Limit - Sugary sodas and energy drinks - Fruit juices with added sugar - Excessive alcohol (1 drink/day women, 2 drinks/day men) - High-caffeine beverages ## Healthy Eating Patterns ### Mediterranean Diet - Rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts - Olive oil as primary fat source - Moderate fish and poultry - Limited red meat - Associated with reduced heart disease, diabetes, and cancer risk ### DASH Diet - Designed to lower blood pressure - High in fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Low in sodium, red meat, added sugars - Rich in potassium, calcium, magnesium ## Dietary Tips for Specific Conditions ### For Diabetes - Monitor carbohydrate intake - Choose low glycemic index foods - Eat regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat", ''relevance_score'': 0.6709153010264636}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6690506386573921}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.668653936037364}]','2026-07-28 13:33:19.794499');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(33,7,'user','How to manage high blood pressure?','','2026-07-28 13:34:46.889090');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(34,7,'assistant',unistr('Managing high blood pressure (hypertension) involves a combination of **lifestyle modifications** and, in some cases, **medications**. Here’s a comprehensive approach based on medical guidelines:\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **1. Lifestyle Modifications**\u000a#### **Dietary Changes**\u000a- **Follow the DASH Diet** (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension):\u000a  - Rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, and low-fat dairy.\u000a  - Includes lean proteins (fish, poultry, beans).\u000a  - Limits saturated fats, cholesterol, and trans fats.\u000a- **Reduce Sodium Intake**:\u000a  - Aim for **<2,300 mg/day** (ideally **<1,500 mg/day** for better control).\u000a  - Avoid processed foods, canned soups, and salty snacks.\u000a- **Increase Potassium-Rich Foods**:\u000a  - Bananas, spinach, sweet potatoes, avocados, and beans help balance sodium levels.\u000a- **Limit Alcohol**:\u000a  - **≤1 drink/day for women**, **≤2 drinks/day for men**.\u000a- **Reduce Caffeine**:\u000a  - Monitor its effect on your blood pressure.\u000a\u000a#### **Physical Activity**\u000a- **Aerobic Exercise**:\u000a  - **150 minutes/week of moderate activity** (e.g., brisk walking, cycling, swimming).\u000a  - **75 minutes/week of vigorous activity** (e.g., running, HIIT).\u000a- **Strength Training**:\u000a  - **2+ days/week** (e.g., weightlifting, resistance bands).\u000a- **Consistency is Key**:\u000a  - Regular exercise helps lower blood pressure over time.\u000a\u000a#### **Weight Management**\u000a- **Maintain a Healthy BMI (18.5–24.9)**:\u000a  - Losing even **5–10% of body weight** can significantly reduce blood pressure.\u000a- **Waist Circumference**:\u000a  - **Men: <40 inches**, **Women: <35 inches** (higher risk if exceeded).\u000a\u000a#### **Stress Management**\u000a- **Relaxation Techniques**:\u000a  - Deep breathing, meditation, yoga, or tai chi.\u000a- **Adequate Sleep**:\u000a  - **7–9 hours/night** (poor sleep can raise blood pressure).\u000a- **Limit Stimulants**:\u000a  - Reduce nicotine (smoking/vaping) and excessive caffeine.\u000a\u000a#### **Other Lifestyle Adjustments**\u000a- **Quit Smoking**:\u000a  - Smoking damages blood vessels and raises blood pressure.\u000a- **Monitor Blood Pressure at Home**:\u000a  - Track readings to share with your doctor.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **2. Medications (If Prescribed by a Doctor)**\u000aIf lifestyle changes aren’t enough, your doctor may prescribe:\u000a- **Diuretics** (e.g., hydrochlorothiazide) – Reduce fluid buildup.\u000a- **ACE Inhibitors** (e.g., lisinopril) – Relax blood vessels.\u000a- **ARBs** (e.g., losartan) – Block blood vessel-constricting hormones.\u000a- **Calcium Channel Blockers** (e.g., amlodipine) – Widen blood vessels.\u000a- **Beta-Blockers** (e.g., metoprolol) – Reduce heart workload.\u000a\u000a**Important**:\u000a- **Never stop or adjust medications without consulting your doctor**.\u000a- Some medications may have side effects (e.g., dizziness, cough).\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **3. Regular Monitoring & Follow-Up**\u000a- **Check Blood Pressure Regularly**:\u000a  - **Normal**: <120/80 mmHg.\u000a  - **Elevated**: 120–129/<80 mmHg.\u000a  - **Stage 1 Hypertension**: 130–139 **or** 80–89 mmHg.\u000a  - **Stage 2 Hypertension**: ≥140 **or** ≥90 mmHg.\u000a  - **Hypertensive Crisis**: ≥180 **and/or** ≥120 mmHg (**seek emergency care**).\u000a- **Visit Your Doctor**:\u000a  - Every **3–6 months** if stable, more often if uncontrolled.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **4. When to Seek Emergency Care**\u000aGo to the ER if you experience:\u000a- **Blood pressure ≥180/120 mmHg** (hypertensive crisis).\u000a- **Severe headache, chest pain, shortness of breath, or confusion** (signs of organ damage).\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Key Takeaways**\u000a✅ **Lifestyle changes** (diet, exercise, stress management) are the **first line of defense**.\u000a✅ **Medications** may be needed if lifestyle adjustments aren’t enough.\u000a✅ **Monitor regularly** and follow up with your doctor.\u000a✅ **Act fast** if blood pressure spikes dangerously.\u000a\u000a**Disclaimer**: *I'),'[{''title'': ''hypertension'', ''content'': ''# Hypertension (High Blood Pressure) ## What is Hypertension? Hypertension, also known as high blood pressure, is a long-term medical condition in which the blood pressure in the arteries is persistently elevated. High blood pressure typically does not cause symptoms. Long-term high blood pressure, however, is a major risk factor for stroke, heart attack, heart failure, peripheral arterial disease, vision loss, chronic kidney disease, and dementia. ## Blood Pressure Categories ### Normal Blood Pressure - Systolic: less than 120 mmHg - Diastolic: less than 80 mmHg ### Elevated Blood Pressure - Systolic: 120-129 mmHg - Diastolic: less than 80 mmHg ### High Blood Pressure Stage 1 - Systolic: 130-139 mmHg - Diastolic: 80-89 mmHg ### High Blood Pressure Stage 2 - Systolic: 140 or higher mmHg - Diastolic: 90 or higher mmHg ### Hypertensive Crisis - Systolic: higher than 180 mmHg - Diastolic: higher than 120 mmHg ## Symptoms Hypertension is often called the "silent killer" because it usually has no symptoms. Some people with very high blood pressure may experience: - Headaches - Shortness of breath - Nosebleeds - Dizziness - Chest pain - Visual changes - Blood in urine ## Risk Factors - Age (risk increases with age) - Family history of hypertension - Being overweight or obese - Lack of physical activity - High sodium diet - Low potassium diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Stress - Chronic kidney disease - Sleep apnea - Certain medications (NSAIDs, birth control pills, decongestants) ## Management ### Lifestyle Modifications - DASH diet (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) - Reduce sodium intake to less than 2,300 mg per day - Regular aerobic exercise (30 minutes most days) - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Limit alcohol to 1 drink per day for women, 2 for men - Quit smoking - Manage stress through relaxation techniques - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) ### Medications - ACE inhibitors (lisinopril, enalapril) - ARBs (losartan, valsartan) - Calcium channel blockers (amlodipine, nifedipine) - Thiazide diuretics (hydrochlorothiazide) - Beta-blockers (metoprolol, atenolol) ## Complications of Untreated Hypertension - Heart attack or heart failure - Stroke - Kidney damage or failure - Vision loss - Peripheral artery disease - Vascular dementia - Hypertensive emergency ## When to Seek Medical Attention If your blood pressure reading is 180/120 mmHg or higher, wait five minutes and test again. If it is still elevated, contact your doctor immediately. A blood pressure reading this high can lead to a stroke or organ damage.'', ''relevance_score'': 0.820837284346683}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.7686418204495863}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': ''regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat mindfully and slowly - Include protein at every meal - Eat plenty of fiber-rich foods - Limit processed and high-calorie foods'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7527954974833045}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.7476319803631188}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "# Heart Disease - Overview ## What is Heart Disease? Heart disease, also known as cardiovascular disease, refers to conditions that involve narrowed or blocked blood vessels that can lead to a heart attack, chest pain (angina), or stroke. It is the leading cause of death worldwide. Common heart diseases include coronary artery disease, heart failure, arrhythmias, and valve problems. ## Types of Heart Disease ### Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) The most common type of heart disease. CAD occurs when the coronary arteries become narrowed or blocked due to plaque buildup (atherosclerosis). This reduces blood flow to the heart muscle and can lead to heart attack. ### Heart Failure Heart failure means the heart cannot pump blood as well as it should. Despite the name, it does not mean the heart has stopped working. It means the heart is not pumping as well as the body needs. ### Arrhythmias Arrhythmias are irregular heartbeats. The heart may beat too quickly (tachycardia), too slowly (bradycardia), or with an irregular pattern. ### Heart Valve Disease When one or more heart valves do not work properly, the heart must work harder to pump blood through the body. ## Risk Factors ### Non-Modifiable Risk Factors - Age (men over 45, women over 55) - Gender (men at higher risk at younger ages) - Family history of heart disease - Race/ethnicity (higher risk for certain populations) ### Modifiable Risk Factors - High blood pressure (hypertension) - High cholesterol - Diabetes - Obesity (BMI > 30) - Smoking - Physical inactivity - Unhealthy diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Chronic stress ## Warning Signs ### Heart Attack Symptoms - Chest pain or discomfort (pressure, squeezing, fullness) - Pain in one or both arms, the back, neck, jaw, or stomach - Shortness of breath - Cold sweat, nausea, lightheadedness - Women may experience atypical symptoms: unusual fatigue, indigestion-like discomfort ### Heart Failure Symptoms - Shortness of breath during activity or when lying down - Fatigue and weakness - Swelling in legs, ankles, and feet - Rapid or irregular heartbeat - Reduced ability to exercise - Persistent cough or wheezing - Swelling of abdomen - Rapid weight gain from fluid retention ## Diagnosis - Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) - Echocardiogram - Stress test - Cardiac catheterization - Blood tests (troponin, BNP, cholesterol) - Coronary angiogram - CT scan or MRI ## Prevention ### Diet - Heart-healthy diet (DASH or Mediterranean) - Limit saturated fat to less than 10% of calories - Limit sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Eat plenty of fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Choose lean proteins - Include omega-3 rich foods ### Exercise - At least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week - Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs", ''relevance_score'': 0.7442990476216669}]','2026-07-28 13:35:03.319600');
