 EAR R. EAR (STOOL) - HELMINTHES CHEST X - RAY - SALMONELLA/SHIGELLA PULMONARY TUBERCULOSIS - V.CHOLERA (SYSTEMIC EXAMINATION) - OTHER BLOOD PRESSURE (BLOOD) HEART - HEMOGLOBIN LUNGS - MALARIA FILM ABDOMEN - OTHERS (OTHERS) (SEROLOGY) *HERNIA - HIV TEST *VARICOSE VEINS EXTREMITIES -F.B.S. SKIN - HBSAG/ANTI HCV (VENEREAL DISEASES VDRL TPHA PREGNANCY TEST CONFIRM IF THE APPLICATION HAS ONE OF THE FOLLOWING: NO YES COMMUNICABLE DISEASES MENTAL DISORDER MENTAL RETARDATION PHYSICAL DISORDERS HANDICAP PARALYSIS BLINDNESS HEARING DISORDER SPEECH DISORDER MENTIONED ABOVE IS THE MEDICAL REPORT FOR MR/ MRS/ MISS » WHOIS [ ] FIT [ ] UNFIT FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED JOB. -TO BE FIT, ALL MEDICAL EXAMINATIONS AND LABORATORY INVESTIGATIONS MUST BE WITHIN NORMAL LIMITS. IN THE EVENT OF AN ABNORMAL/POSITIVE RESULT, A TYPEWRITTEN LETTER SIGNED BY THE PHYSICIAN STATING THE CONDITION AND ANY TREATMENT IMPLEMENTED. THIS LETTER SHOULD ALSO INDICATE WHETHER THIS CONDITION OR TREATMENT WILL HAVE ANY EFFECT ON THE APPLICANT’S WORK. PHYSICIAN NAME: SIGNATURE: LICENSE NUMBER: STAMP: THIS FORM MUST BE ATTESTED BY ONE OF THE TWO FOLLOWING AUTHORITIES: DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH THIS IS TO CERTIFY THAT DR. LICENSE NUMBER: i (2) IS CURRENTLY LICENSED TO PRACTICE MEDICINE. qd) AUTHORIZED SIGNATURE : STAMP OR SEAL OF THE STATE AUTHORITY (COLLEGE OF PHYSICIANS) SUBMIT TO THE CONSULAR SECTION THREE ORIGINALS COPIES OF THIS MEDICAL REPORT AND TWO COPIES OF ALL RESULTS OF THE MEDICAL TESTS. DO NOT SUBMIT X-RAYS AS THOSE MUST BE PRESENTED TO THE HEALTH AUTHORITIES IN SAUDI ARABIA ALONG WITH ONE CLEAR COPY OF THIS REPORT AND ALL TEST RESULTS.'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7089726379534882}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': "# Fever, Cold, and Influenza (Flu) ## Fever ### What is Fever? Fever is an increase in the body''s temperature, which is regulated by the hypothalamus. A normal body temperature is approximately 98.6°F (37°C). A fever is generally considered to be a temperature of 100.4°F (38°C) or higher. ### Causes of Fever - Infections (bacterial, viral, fungal) - Inflammatory conditions - Medications - Heat stroke - Certain cancers - Autoimmune disorders ### When to See a Doctor - Infants under 3 months: any fever over 100.4°F (38°C) - Children 3 months to 3 years: fever of 102.2°F (39°C) or higher - Adults: fever above 103°F (39.4°C) or fever lasting more than 3 days - Anyone with fever accompanied by severe headache, stiff neck, confusion, or rash ### Fever Management - Stay hydrated by drinking plenty of fluids - Rest adequately - Dress in lightweight clothing - Take acetaminophen or ibuprofen (as directed) - Lukewarm sponge bath if temperature is very high - Monitor temperature regularly ## Common Cold ### What is a Cold? The common cold is a viral infection of your nose and throat (upper respiratory tract). It is usually harmless, although it might not feel that way. Many types of viruses can cause a cold, but rhinoviruses are the most common. ### Symptoms - Runny or stuffy nose - Sore throat - Cough - Congestion - Slight body aches - Sneezing - Low-grade fever - Generally feeling unwell (malaise) ### Duration - Onset: 1-3 days after exposure - Duration: 7-10 days - Cough may persist for 2 weeks ### Treatment - Rest - Stay hydrated - Saline nasal drops or spray - Warm liquids (tea, broth) - Honey (for adults and children over 1 year) - Over-the-counter cold medications (for adults) - Gargle with warm salt water for sore throat ## Influenza (Flu) ### What is the Flu? Influenza (flu) is a contagious respiratory illness caused by influenza viruses. It can cause mild to severe illness, and at times can lead to death. Flu is different from a cold. ### Flu vs Cold The flu and the common cold have similar symptoms, but the flu is generally more severe: - Flu: sudden onset, high fever (100-104°F), severe body aches, extreme fatigue - Cold: gradual onset, low or no fever, mild aches, mild fatigue ### Flu Symptoms - Fever (usually high) - Severe body aches - Headache - Fatigue (can last 2-3 weeks) - Dry cough - Sore throat - Nasal congestion - Chills - Sweating ### When to Seek Emergency Care - Difficulty breathing or shortness of breath - Persistent pain or pressure in chest - Sudden dizziness or confusion - Severe or persistent vomiting - Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours -", ''relevance_score'': 0.7077367890395586}]','2026-07-29 07:01:08.371926');
