INSERT INTO messages VALUES(48,11,'assistant',unistr('I am an AI assistant for informational support only. **Not a substitute for professional medical advice.** Always consult a healthcare professional before taking any medication.\u000a\u000aSince you''re experiencing **headaches with repeated vomiting**, here are some **general guidelines** on what may help—**but you should see a doctor for proper diagnosis and treatment.**\u000a\u000a### **Possible Over-the-Counter (OTC) Medications (If No Contraindications)**\u000a1. **For Headache & Nausea:**\u000a   - **Acetaminophen (Paracetamol)** – Helps with mild to moderate headaches (avoid if liver issues).\u000a   - **Ibuprofen or Naproxen** – Anti-inflammatory pain relief (avoid if stomach ulcers, kidney problems, or dehydration).\u000a   - **Antiemetics (Anti-Nausea Meds):**\u000a     - **Dimenhydrinate (Dramamine)** – For motion sickness-related nausea.\u000a     - **Meclizine (Bonine)** – Helps with vertigo/dizziness-related nausea.\u000a     - **Doxylamine (Unisom)** – Sometimes used for nausea (check with a doctor first).\u000a\u000a2. **For Migraine (If Diagnosed):**\u000a   - **Triptans (Sumatriptan, Rizatriptan)** – Prescription-only for migraines.\u000a   - **NSAIDs + Caffeine (Excedrin Migraine)** – May help mild migraines.\u000a\u000a3. **For Dehydration (Due to Vomiting):**\u000a   - **Oral Rehydration Solutions (ORS)** – Better than plain water (contains electrolytes).\u000a   - **Electrolyte drinks (Pedialyte, Gatorade in moderation).**\u000a\u000a### **⚠️ Important Warnings:**\u000a- **Do NOT take painkillers on an empty stomach** (can worsen nausea).\u000a- **Avoid aspirin if vomiting blood or if you have stomach issues.**\u000a- **If vomiting persists, do NOT take oral meds** (they may not absorb properly).\u000a- **If you suspect a serious condition (e.g., meningitis, brain pressure, poisoning), seek emergency care immediately.**\u000a\u000a### **When to See a Doctor Urgently:**\u000a✔ **Vomiting lasts more than 24 hours**\u000a✔ **Severe headache with fever, stiff neck, or confusion**\u000a✔ **Blood in vomit or black stools**\u000a✔ **Signs of dehydration (dizziness, dark urine, extreme thirst)**\u000a✔ **Headache worsens when lying down or with coughing/sneezing**\u000a\u000a### **Non-Medication Relief Tips:**\u000a- **Rest in a dark, quiet room** (helps migraines).\u000a- **Apply a cold compress to the forehead or neck.**\u000a- **Sip small amounts of water or ginger tea** (ginger may help nausea).\u000a- **Avoid strong smells, bright lights, and loud noises.**\u000a\u000a**Final Advice:** Since your symptoms could be due to multiple causes (migraine, infection, dehydration, or something more serious), **please consult a doctor for proper evaluation.** If symptoms are severe, **go to the ER immediately.**\u000a\u000aWould you like help finding nearby medical services?'),'[{''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6638610912099504}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6601326749921301}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6573425912681613}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.6304849745207408}, {''title'': ''Medical-report-1-2048'', ''content'': ''a MEDICAL REPORT = (2 8 cigs NAME: NATIONALITY: | SEX: | AGE: MARITAL STATUS: PASSPORT NO: | ISSUE PLACE: | ISSUE DATE: POSITION APPLIED FOR: PHOTO DEAR SIR/ MADAM PLEASE, ARRANGE TO EXAMINE THE ABOVE MENTIONED CANDIDATE AS TO HIS/HER FITNESS FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED POSITION. DATE / / RECRUITMENT ATTACHE/OR DOCTOR: HISTORY OF ANY SIGNIFICANT PAST ILLNESS INCLUDING: - PSYCHIATRIC AND NEUROLOGICAL DISORDERS (EPILEPSY, DEPRESSION...) - ALLERGY MEDICAL EXAMINATION LABORATORY INVESTIGATION TYPE OF MEDICAL EXAMINATION NEGATIVE\\ POSITIVE\\ TYPE OF LABORATORY NEGATIVE\\ POSITIVE\\ NORMAL ABNORMAL INVESTIGATION NORMAL ABNORMAL VISION R. EYE (URINE) L. EYE - SUGAR EYE - ALBUMIN OTHER R. EYE - BILHARZIASIS L. EYE - OTHER EAR R. EAR (STOOL) - HELMINTHES CHEST X - RAY - SALMONELLA/SHIGELLA PULMONARY TUBERCULOSIS - V.CHOLERA (SYSTEMIC EXAMINATION) - OTHER BLOOD PRESSURE (BLOOD) HEART - HEMOGLOBIN LUNGS - MALARIA FILM ABDOMEN - OTHERS (OTHERS) (SEROLOGY) *HERNIA - HIV TEST *VARICOSE VEINS EXTREMITIES -F.B.S. SKIN - HBSAG/ANTI HCV (VENEREAL DISEASES VDRL TPHA PREGNANCY TEST CONFIRM IF THE APPLICATION HAS ONE OF THE FOLLOWING: NO YES COMMUNICABLE DISEASES MENTAL DISORDER MENTAL RETARDATION PHYSICAL DISORDERS HANDICAP PARALYSIS BLINDNESS HEARING DISORDER SPEECH DISORDER MENTIONED ABOVE IS THE MEDICAL REPORT FOR MR/ MRS/ MISS » WHOIS [ ] FIT [ ] UNFIT FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED JOB. -TO BE FIT, ALL MEDICAL EXAMINATIONS AND LABORATORY INVESTIGATIONS MUST BE WITHIN NORMAL LIMITS. IN THE EVENT OF AN ABNORMAL/POSITIVE RESULT, A TYPEWRITTEN LETTER SIGNED BY THE PHYSICIAN STATING THE CONDITION AND ANY TREATMENT IMPLEMENTED. THIS LETTER SHOULD ALSO INDICATE WHETHER THIS CONDITION OR TREATMENT WILL HAVE ANY EFFECT ON THE APPLICANT’S WORK. PHYSICIAN NAME: SIGNATURE: LICENSE NUMBER: STAMP: THIS FORM MUST BE ATTESTED BY ONE OF THE TWO FOLLOWING AUTHORITIES: DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH THIS IS TO CERTIFY THAT DR. LICENSE NUMBER: i (2) IS CURRENTLY LICENSED TO PRACTICE MEDICINE. qd) AUTHORIZED SIGNATURE : STAMP OR SEAL OF THE STATE AUTHORITY (COLLEGE OF PHYSICIANS) SUBMIT TO THE CONSULAR SECTION THREE ORIGINALS COPIES OF THIS MEDICAL REPORT AND TWO COPIES OF ALL RESULTS OF THE MEDICAL TESTS. DO NOT SUBMIT X-RAYS AS THOSE MUST BE PRESENTED TO THE HEALTH AUTHORITIES IN SAUDI ARABIA ALONG WITH ONE CLEAR COPY OF THIS REPORT AND ALL TEST RESULTS.'', ''relevance_score'': 0.623984958732128}]','2026-07-29 07:02:31.316013');
