INSERT INTO messages VALUES(52,11,'assistant',unistr('I am an AI assistant for **informational support only** and **cannot provide personalized medical recommendations, including specific doctor or surgeon referrals**. However, I can guide you on **how to find the best specialists** in India for **neurological or emergency conditions** causing **headaches with vomiting**.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **🔍 How to Find the Best Surgeons/Doctors for Your Condition in India**\u000aSince your symptoms (**headache + vomiting**) could stem from **neurological issues (e.g., brain tumors, aneurysms, infections), gastrointestinal problems, or other serious conditions**, you’ll need a **neurosurgeon, neurologist, or emergency specialist** depending on the diagnosis.\u000a\u000a#### **1. Specialists to Consult (Based on Possible Causes)**\u000a| **Possible Cause**          | **Specialist to See**               | **When to Consider Surgery?** |\u000a|-----------------------------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------|\u000a| **Migraine/Severe Headaches** | Neurologist                        | Rarely (only if chronic & unresponsive to meds) |\u000a| **Brain Tumor**             | **Neurosurgeon**                   | **Yes** (if tumor is operable) |\u000a| **Aneurysm (Brain Bleed)**  | **Neurosurgeon / Vascular Surgeon** | **Yes** (emergency clipping/coiling) |\u000a| **Meningitis/Encephalitis** | Neurologist / Infectious Disease   | **No** (treated with meds) |\u000a| **Hydrocephalus**           | **Neurosurgeon**                   | **Yes** (shunt surgery) |\u000a| **Severe Sinusitis**        | ENT Surgeon                        | **Yes** (if chronic & blocked) |\u000a| **Gastrointestinal Issues** | Gastroenterologist                 | **Rarely** (unless obstruction) |\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **🏆 Top Hospitals with Leading Neurosurgeons in India**\u000aThese hospitals have **world-class neurosurgery departments** and are known for treating **brain tumors, aneurysms, hydrocephalus, and complex neurological conditions**:\u000a\u000a#### **📍 Delhi/NCR**\u000a1. **All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), New Delhi**\u000a   - **Best for:** Government-funded top neurosurgeons\u000a   - **Key Surgeons (Past/Present):**\u000a     - **Dr. P. Sarat Chandra** (Neuro-oncology, epilepsy surgery)\u000a     - **Dr. Ashok Kumar Mahapatra** (Former HOD, Neurosurgery)\u000a     - **Dr. Deepak Gupta** (Skull base & vascular neurosurgery)\u000a   - **Website:** [www.aiims.edu](https://www.aiims.edu)\u000a\u000a2. **Medanta - The Medicity, Gurgaon**\u000a   - **Best for:** Private-sector advanced neurosurgery\u000a   - **Key Surgeons:**\u000a     - **Dr. Sandeep Vaishya** (Minimally invasive neurosurgery, brain tumors)\u000a     - **Dr. V.S. Mehta** (Padma Bhushan awardee, skull base surgery)\u000a   - **Website:** [www.medanta.org](https://www.medanta.org)\u000a\u000a3. **Fortis Memorial Research Institute (FMRI), Gurgaon**\u000a   - **Best for:** Robotic & stereotactic neurosurgery\u000a   - **Key Surgeons:**\u000a     - **Dr. Rana Patir** (Neuro-oncology, spine surgery)\u000a     - **Dr. Anil Kumar Kansal** (Vascular neurosurgery)\u000a   - **Website:** [www.fortishealthcare.com](https://www.fortishealthcare.com)\u000a\u000a4. **Indraprastha Apollo Hospitals, Delhi**\u000a   - **Best for:** Comprehensive neurosurgery & emergency care\u000a   - **Key Surgeons:**\u000a     - **Dr. B.K. Misra** (Gamma Knife radiosurgery, brain tumors)\u000a     - **Dr. Rana Saha** (Skull base & vascular neurosurgery)\u000a   - **Website:** [www.apollohospitals.com](https://www.apollohospitals.com)\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### **📍 Mumbai**\u000a5. **Tata Memorial Hospital (TMH), Mumbai**\u000a   - **Best for:** Neuro-oncology (brain tumors)\u000a   - **Key Surgeons:**\u000a     - **Dr. Aliasgar Moiyadi** (Neuro-oncology, skull base surgery)\u000a     - **Dr. Tejpal Gupta** (Radiation oncology for brain tumors)\u000a   - **Website:** [www.tmc.gov.in](https://tmc.gov.in)\u000a\u000a6. **Kokilaben Dhirubhai Ambani Hospital, Mumbai**\u000a   - **Best for:** Minimally invasive neurosurgery\u000a   - **Key Surgeons:**'),'[{''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6296227545933523}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6147362255517083}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.6024773190806547}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "# Heart Disease - Overview ## What is Heart Disease? Heart disease, also known as cardiovascular disease, refers to conditions that involve narrowed or blocked blood vessels that can lead to a heart attack, chest pain (angina), or stroke. It is the leading cause of death worldwide. Common heart diseases include coronary artery disease, heart failure, arrhythmias, and valve problems. ## Types of Heart Disease ### Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) The most common type of heart disease. CAD occurs when the coronary arteries become narrowed or blocked due to plaque buildup (atherosclerosis). This reduces blood flow to the heart muscle and can lead to heart attack. ### Heart Failure Heart failure means the heart cannot pump blood as well as it should. Despite the name, it does not mean the heart has stopped working. It means the heart is not pumping as well as the body needs. ### Arrhythmias Arrhythmias are irregular heartbeats. The heart may beat too quickly (tachycardia), too slowly (bradycardia), or with an irregular pattern. ### Heart Valve Disease When one or more heart valves do not work properly, the heart must work harder to pump blood through the body. ## Risk Factors ### Non-Modifiable Risk Factors - Age (men over 45, women over 55) - Gender (men at higher risk at younger ages) - Family history of heart disease - Race/ethnicity (higher risk for certain populations) ### Modifiable Risk Factors - High blood pressure (hypertension) - High cholesterol - Diabetes - Obesity (BMI > 30) - Smoking - Physical inactivity - Unhealthy diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Chronic stress ## Warning Signs ### Heart Attack Symptoms - Chest pain or discomfort (pressure, squeezing, fullness) - Pain in one or both arms, the back, neck, jaw, or stomach - Shortness of breath - Cold sweat, nausea, lightheadedness - Women may experience atypical symptoms: unusual fatigue, indigestion-like discomfort ### Heart Failure Symptoms - Shortness of breath during activity or when lying down - Fatigue and weakness - Swelling in legs, ankles, and feet - Rapid or irregular heartbeat - Reduced ability to exercise - Persistent cough or wheezing - Swelling of abdomen - Rapid weight gain from fluid retention ## Diagnosis - Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) - Echocardiogram - Stress test - Cardiac catheterization - Blood tests (troponin, BNP, cholesterol) - Coronary angiogram - CT scan or MRI ## Prevention ### Diet - Heart-healthy diet (DASH or Mediterranean) - Limit saturated fat to less than 10% of calories - Limit sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Eat plenty of fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Choose lean proteins - Include omega-3 rich foods ### Exercise - At least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week - Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs", ''relevance_score'': 0.5933514682768689}, {''title'': ''Medical-report-1-2048'', ''content'': ''a MEDICAL REPORT = (2 8 cigs NAME: NATIONALITY: | SEX: | AGE: MARITAL STATUS: PASSPORT NO: | ISSUE PLACE: | ISSUE DATE: POSITION APPLIED FOR: PHOTO DEAR SIR/ MADAM PLEASE, ARRANGE TO EXAMINE THE ABOVE MENTIONED CANDIDATE AS TO HIS/HER FITNESS FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED POSITION. DATE / / RECRUITMENT ATTACHE/OR DOCTOR: HISTORY OF ANY SIGNIFICANT PAST ILLNESS INCLUDING: - PSYCHIATRIC AND NEUROLOGICAL DISORDERS (EPILEPSY, DEPRESSION...) - ALLERGY MEDICAL EXAMINATION LABORATORY INVESTIGATION TYPE OF MEDICAL EXAMINATION NEGATIVE\\ POSITIVE\\ TYPE OF LABORATORY NEGATIVE\\ POSITIVE\\ NORMAL ABNORMAL INVESTIGATION NORMAL ABNORMAL VISION R. EYE (URINE) L. EYE - SUGAR EYE - ALBUMIN OTHER R. EYE - BILHARZIASIS L. EYE - OTHER EAR R. EAR (STOOL) - HELMINTHES CHEST X - RAY - SALMONELLA/SHIGELLA PULMONARY TUBERCULOSIS - V.CHOLERA (SYSTEMIC EXAMINATION) - OTHER BLOOD PRESSURE (BLOOD) HEART - HEMOGLOBIN LUNGS - MALARIA FILM ABDOMEN - OTHERS (OTHERS) (SEROLOGY) *HERNIA - HIV TEST *VARICOSE VEINS EXTREMITIES -F.B.S. SKIN - HBSAG/ANTI HCV (VENEREAL DISEASES VDRL TPHA PREGNANCY TEST CONFIRM IF THE APPLICATION HAS ONE OF THE FOLLOWING: NO YES COMMUNICABLE DISEASES MENTAL DISORDER MENTAL RETARDATION PHYSICAL DISORDERS HANDICAP PARALYSIS BLINDNESS HEARING DISORDER SPEECH DISORDER MENTIONED ABOVE IS THE MEDICAL REPORT FOR MR/ MRS/ MISS » WHOIS [ ] FIT [ ] UNFIT FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED JOB. -TO BE FIT, ALL MEDICAL EXAMINATIONS AND LABORATORY INVESTIGATIONS MUST BE WITHIN NORMAL LIMITS. IN THE EVENT OF AN ABNORMAL/POSITIVE RESULT, A TYPEWRITTEN LETTER SIGNED BY THE PHYSICIAN STATING THE CONDITION AND ANY TREATMENT IMPLEMENTED. THIS LETTER SHOULD ALSO INDICATE WHETHER THIS CONDITION OR TREATMENT WILL HAVE ANY EFFECT ON THE APPLICANT’S WORK. PHYSICIAN NAME: SIGNATURE: LICENSE NUMBER: STAMP: THIS FORM MUST BE ATTESTED BY ONE OF THE TWO FOLLOWING AUTHORITIES: DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH THIS IS TO CERTIFY THAT DR. LICENSE NUMBER: i (2) IS CURRENTLY LICENSED TO PRACTICE MEDICINE. qd) AUTHORIZED SIGNATURE : STAMP OR SEAL OF THE STATE AUTHORITY (COLLEGE OF PHYSICIANS) SUBMIT TO THE CONSULAR SECTION THREE ORIGINALS COPIES OF THIS MEDICAL REPORT AND TWO COPIES OF ALL RESULTS OF THE MEDICAL TESTS. DO NOT SUBMIT X-RAYS AS THOSE MUST BE PRESENTED TO THE HEALTH AUTHORITIES IN SAUDI ARABIA ALONG WITH ONE CLEAR COPY OF THIS REPORT AND ALL TEST RESULTS.'', ''relevance_score'': 0.5847830849846328}]','2026-07-29 07:04:49.245178');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(53,12,'user','i have high fever and heavy back pain i ate street foods what disease will be there','','2026-07-29 07:08:24.174035');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(54,12,'assistant',unistr('I’m sorry to hear you’re not feeling well. Based on your symptoms—**high fever and heavy back pain**—along with a history of eating **street food**, here are some possible medical conditions to consider. However, **this is not a diagnosis**, and you should consult a healthcare professional immediately for proper evaluation and treatment.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Possible Conditions to Consider**\u000a\u000a#### 1. **Typhoid Fever (Enteric Fever)**\u000a   - **Cause**: Bacterial infection (*Salmonella Typhi* or *Salmonella Paratyphi*) often spread through contaminated food or water.\u000a   - **Symptoms**:\u000a     - High fever (can rise gradually and persist for days or weeks)\u000a     - Weakness, fatigue\u000a     - Headache\u000a     - **Back pain** (common in typhoid)\u000a     - Abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea (though some people may have constipation)\u000a     - Loss of appetite\u000a     - Rose-colored spots on the chest (in some cases)\u000a   - **Why it fits?**\u000a     Street food (especially undercooked or raw items like salads, chutneys, or unpeeled fruits) is a common source of typhoid infection. Back pain is a frequent symptom due to muscle inflammation or systemic infection.\u000a\u000a#### 2. **Dengue Fever**\u000a   - **Cause**: Viral infection spread by *Aedes* mosquitoes (not directly from food, but mosquitoes breed in stagnant water, which can be near street food stalls).\u000a   - **Symptoms**:\u000a     - Sudden high fever (104°F/40°C or higher)\u000a     - Severe **back pain** (often described as "breakbone fever" due to intense muscle and joint pain)\u000a     - Headache (especially behind the eyes)\u000a     - Nausea, vomiting\u000a     - Skin rash (may appear 3-4 days after fever starts)\u000a     - Fatigue\u000a   - **Why it fits?**\u000a     If you were in an area with poor sanitation or standing water (common near street food stalls), mosquito exposure is possible. Back pain is a hallmark of dengue.\u000a\u000a#### 3. **Leptospirosis**\u000a   - **Cause**: Bacterial infection (*Leptospira*) spread through water or food contaminated with the urine of infected animals (e.g., rats).\u000a   - **Symptoms**:\u000a     - High fever\u000a     - **Severe muscle pain (especially in the back and calves)**\u000a     - Headache\u000a     - Chills\u000a     - Jaundice (yellow skin/eyes) in severe cases\u000a     - Red eyes\u000a     - Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea\u000a   - **Why it fits?**\u000a     Street food stalls with poor hygiene (e.g., uncovered food, rodent infestations) can expose you to *Leptospira*. Back pain is a key symptom.\u000a\u000a#### 4. **Food Poisoning (Severe Bacterial Infection)**\u000a   - **Cause**: Bacteria like *E. coli*, *Salmonella*, *Shigella*, or *Campylobacter* from contaminated food.\u000a   - **Symptoms**:\u000a     - High fever (in severe cases)\u000a     - Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea (may be bloody)\u000a     - Abdominal cramps\u000a     - **Back pain** (due to dehydration, muscle strain from vomiting, or referred pain from abdominal organs)\u000a     - Weakness\u000a   - **Why it fits?**\u000a     Street food is a common source of foodborne illnesses. While back pain isn’t a classic symptom, severe dehydration or referred pain from abdominal organs (e.g., kidneys) can cause it.\u000a\u000a#### 5. **Urinary Tract Infection (UTI) or Kidney Infection (Pyelonephritis)**\u000a   - **Cause**: Bacterial infection (often *E. coli*) in the urinary tract, which can spread to the kidneys.\u000a   - **Symptoms**:\u000a     - High fever (especially with kidney infection)\u000a     - **Lower back pain (flank pain)** or side pain\u000a     - Painful or frequent urination\u000a     - Cloudy, bloody, or foul-smelling urine\u000a     - Nausea, vomiting\u000a   - **Why it fits?**\u000a     While UTIs aren’t directly caused by food, dehydration from food poisoning or poor hygiene can increase the risk. Back pain (especially one-sided) is a red flag for kidney involvement.\u000a\u000a#### 6. **Malaria**\u000a   - **Cause**: Parasitic infection (*Plasmodium*) spread by *Anopheles* mosquitoes.\u000a   - **Symptoms**:\u000a     - High fever (often cyclical, with chills and sweating)\u000a     - **Back pain** (common due to muscle aches)\u000a     - Headache\u000a     - Nausea, vomiting\u000a     - Fatigue\u000a     - Jaundice (in severe cases)\u000a   - **Why it fits?**'),'[{''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7317691513006153}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': "# Fever, Cold, and Influenza (Flu) ## Fever ### What is Fever? Fever is an increase in the body''s temperature, which is regulated by the hypothalamus. A normal body temperature is approximately 98.6°F (37°C). A fever is generally considered to be a temperature of 100.4°F (38°C) or higher. ### Causes of Fever - Infections (bacterial, viral, fungal) - Inflammatory conditions - Medications - Heat stroke - Certain cancers - Autoimmune disorders ### When to See a Doctor - Infants under 3 months: any fever over 100.4°F (38°C) - Children 3 months to 3 years: fever of 102.2°F (39°C) or higher - Adults: fever above 103°F (39.4°C) or fever lasting more than 3 days - Anyone with fever accompanied by severe headache, stiff neck, confusion, or rash ### Fever Management - Stay hydrated by drinking plenty of fluids - Rest adequately - Dress in lightweight clothing - Take acetaminophen or ibuprofen (as directed) - Lukewarm sponge bath if temperature is very high - Monitor temperature regularly ## Common Cold ### What is a Cold? The common cold is a viral infection of your nose and throat (upper respiratory tract). It is usually harmless, although it might not feel that way. Many types of viruses can cause a cold, but rhinoviruses are the most common. ### Symptoms - Runny or stuffy nose - Sore throat - Cough - Congestion - Slight body aches - Sneezing - Low-grade fever - Generally feeling unwell (malaise) ### Duration - Onset: 1-3 days after exposure - Duration: 7-10 days - Cough may persist for 2 weeks ### Treatment - Rest - Stay hydrated - Saline nasal drops or spray - Warm liquids (tea, broth) - Honey (for adults and children over 1 year) - Over-the-counter cold medications (for adults) - Gargle with warm salt water for sore throat ## Influenza (Flu) ### What is the Flu? Influenza (flu) is a contagious respiratory illness caused by influenza viruses. It can cause mild to severe illness, and at times can lead to death. Flu is different from a cold. ### Flu vs Cold The flu and the common cold have similar symptoms, but the flu is generally more severe: - Flu: sudden onset, high fever (100-104°F), severe body aches, extreme fatigue - Cold: gradual onset, low or no fever, mild aches, mild fatigue ### Flu Symptoms - Fever (usually high) - Severe body aches - Headache - Fatigue (can last 2-3 weeks) - Dry cough - Sore throat - Nasal congestion - Chills - Sweating ### When to Seek Emergency Care - Difficulty breathing or shortness of breath - Persistent pain or pressure in chest - Sudden dizziness or confusion - Severe or persistent vomiting - Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours -", ''relevance_score'': 0.7263930052846606}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.72426656602293}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "# Heart Disease - Overview ## What is Heart Disease? Heart disease, also known as cardiovascular disease, refers to conditions that involve narrowed or blocked blood vessels that can lead to a heart attack, chest pain (angina), or stroke. It is the leading cause of death worldwide. Common heart diseases include coronary artery disease, heart failure, arrhythmias, and valve problems. ## Types of Heart Disease ### Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) The most common type of heart disease. CAD occurs when the coronary arteries become narrowed or blocked due to plaque buildup (atherosclerosis). This reduces blood flow to the heart muscle and can lead to heart attack. ### Heart Failure Heart failure means the heart cannot pump blood as well as it should. Despite the name, it does not mean the heart has stopped working. It means the heart is not pumping as well as the body needs. ### Arrhythmias Arrhythmias are irregular heartbeats. The heart may beat too quickly (tachycardia), too slowly (bradycardia), or with an irregular pattern. ### Heart Valve Disease When one or more heart valves do not work properly, the heart must work harder to pump blood through the body. ## Risk Factors ### Non-Modifiable Risk Factors - Age (men over 45, women over 55) - Gender (men at higher risk at younger ages) - Family history of heart disease - Race/ethnicity (higher risk for certain populations) ### Modifiable Risk Factors - High blood pressure (hypertension) - High cholesterol - Diabetes - Obesity (BMI > 30) - Smoking - Physical inactivity - Unhealthy diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Chronic stress ## Warning Signs ### Heart Attack Symptoms - Chest pain or discomfort (pressure, squeezing, fullness) - Pain in one or both arms, the back, neck, jaw, or stomach - Shortness of breath - Cold sweat, nausea, lightheadedness - Women may experience atypical symptoms: unusual fatigue, indigestion-like discomfort ### Heart Failure Symptoms - Shortness of breath during activity or when lying down - Fatigue and weakness - Swelling in legs, ankles, and feet - Rapid or irregular heartbeat - Reduced ability to exercise - Persistent cough or wheezing - Swelling of abdomen - Rapid weight gain from fluid retention ## Diagnosis - Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) - Echocardiogram - Stress test - Cardiac catheterization - Blood tests (troponin, BNP, cholesterol) - Coronary angiogram - CT scan or MRI ## Prevention ### Diet - Heart-healthy diet (DASH or Mediterranean) - Limit saturated fat to less than 10% of calories - Limit sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Eat plenty of fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Choose lean proteins - Include omega-3 rich foods ### Exercise - At least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week - Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs", ''relevance_score'': 0.6874841863164854}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.6852256876355234}]','2026-07-29 07:08:49.538369');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(55,13,'user','this a report i got form my lab explain this','','2026-07-29 07:15:52.186189');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(56,13,'assistant',unistr('Based on the histopathology report you provided, here’s a clear explanation of the findings in simple terms:\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Histopathology Report Explanation**\u000aThis report describes the microscopic examination of tissue samples taken from your gastrointestinal (GI) tract, specifically from the **duodenum (first part of the small intestine), gastroesophageal junction (where the esophagus meets the stomach), and distal esophagus (lower part of the esophagus)**. Here’s what the findings mean:\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### **1. Duodenal Mucosa (Small Intestine) Findings:**\u000a- **Nonspecific Duodenitis**:\u000a  - The duodenum shows signs of **inflammation** (duodenitis), but the cause is not specified (hence "nonspecific").\u000a  - This could be due to infections, medications (like NSAIDs), acid reflux, or other irritants. It is usually treatable with dietary changes or medications (e.g., proton pump inhibitors).\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### **2. Tumor Findings (Poorly Cohesive Carcinoma):**\u000aThe report describes a **tumor** in the **greater curvature of the stomach, gastroesophageal junction (GEJ), and distal esophagus**. Here’s what this means:\u000a\u000a- **Poorly Cohesive Carcinoma (PCC)**:\u000a  - This is a type of **stomach (gastric) cancer** where the cancer cells are loosely connected and often have a "signet-ring" appearance under the microscope (due to mucin-filled cytoplasm pushing the nucleus to the side).\u000a  - PCC is a subtype of **adenocarcinoma** (the most common type of stomach cancer) and tends to be more aggressive than other forms. It can spread (metastasize) to other organs like the liver, lymph nodes, or peritoneum (lining of the abdomen).\u000a\u000a- **Location**:\u000a  - The tumor is found in the **greater curvature of the stomach** (the outer, longer curve of the stomach), the **gastroesophageal junction (GEJ)** (where the esophagus meets the stomach), and the **distal esophagus** (lower part of the esophagus).\u000a  - This suggests the cancer may have originated in the stomach and spread upward into the esophagus or vice versa.\u000a\u000a- **Microscopic Features**:\u000a  - The tumor cells are described as **round to oval with eccentrically placed nuclei and vacuolated cytoplasm** (signet-ring cells).\u000a  - There is **extracellular mucin** (mucus outside the cells), which is typical of this cancer type.\u000a  - The tumor grows in **nests and sheets**, which is how pathologists identify its aggressive nature.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### **3. Squamous Epithelium (Esophagus) Findings:**\u000a- The report mentions **squamous epithelium** (the normal lining of the esophagus) with underlying tissue showing tumor infiltration.\u000a  - This suggests the cancer has **invaded the esophagus** from the stomach or GEJ.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **What Does This Mean for You?**\u000a1. **Diagnosis**:\u000a   - You have been diagnosed with **poorly cohesive carcinoma (PCC)**, a type of stomach cancer that may also involve the esophagus. This is a serious condition that requires prompt medical attention.\u000a\u000a2. **Next Steps**:\u000a   - **Staging**: Your doctor will likely order additional tests (e.g., CT scan, PET scan, endoscopic ultrasound) to determine the **stage** of the cancer (how far it has spread). Staging helps guide treatment.\u000a   - **Treatment Options**:\u000a     - **Surgery**: If the cancer is localized, surgery to remove the tumor (e.g., gastrectomy or esophagectomy) may be an option.\u000a     - **Chemotherapy/Radiation**: Often used before or after surgery to shrink the tumor or kill remaining cancer cells.\u000a     - **Targeted Therapy/Immunotherapy**: May be considered depending on the cancer’s genetic profile.\u000a   - **Multidisciplinary Team**: You will likely be referred to a team of specialists, including:\u000a     - A **gastroenterologist** (for endoscopic procedures).\u000a     - An **oncologist** (for cancer treatment).\u000a     - A **surgeon** (if surgery is needed).\u000a     - A **dietitian** (to manage nutrition during treatment).\u000a\u000a3. **Prognosis**:\u000a   - PCC is often diagnosed at a later stage because symptoms (e.g., indigestion, weight loss, abdominal pain) may not appear until the cancer is advanced. However, early detection and treatment can improve outcomes.\u000a   - Prognosis depends on the **stage**, your overall health, and how the cancer responds to treatment. Your doctor will discuss this with you in detail.\u000a\u000a4. **Support**:\u000a   - Cancer treatment can be physically and emotionally challenging. Consider joining a **support group** or seeking counseling to help cope with the diagnosis.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Important Notes:**\u000a- This report is a **consensus opinion** from multiple pathologists, meaning it'),'[{''title'': ''WhatsApp Image 2026-07-29 at 12.44.29 (1)'', ''content'': ''MISTORATHOLOGICAL EXAMINATION TEST Clinical Date Specimen ~ oss Pathology 2. Specime . yrey-white soft tissue bits. A/E ~ Lblock.B Specim sts of mutliple grey-white soft tissue bits. A/E - 1block A-Se how duodenal mucosa with lamina propria showing lymphocy ic mucosa with a tumour composed of round t a trically placed nuclei and vacuolated cytoplasm. The tumour 1 pth and sheets, Extra cellular mucin seen, show squamous epithelium with underlying tissue infiltrate he ” a BC - Poorly Cohesive Carcinoma, Greater curvature, og junction and distal esophagus. age A Nonspecific duodenitis, emarks tus: Final Report tupathological exammation report is @ consensus opinion given by multiple experienced ased un the received tissue material and slides given fur examination. and slides wil be stored for 3 years. ns will be stored for 2 months. *** End of Report *#* ie isonet eA A OO ea a'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7660092622257743}, {''title'': ''Medical-report-1-2048'', ''content'': ''a MEDICAL REPORT = (2 8 cigs NA