E DISEASES MENTAL DISORDER MENTAL RETARDATION PHYSICAL DISORDERS HANDICAP PARALYSIS BLINDNESS HEARING DISORDER SPEECH DISORDER MENTIONED ABOVE IS THE MEDICAL REPORT FOR MR/ MRS/ MISS » WHOIS [ ] FIT [ ] UNFIT FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED JOB. -TO BE FIT, ALL MEDICAL EXAMINATIONS AND LABORATORY INVESTIGATIONS MUST BE WITHIN NORMAL LIMITS. IN THE EVENT OF AN ABNORMAL/POSITIVE RESULT, A TYPEWRITTEN LETTER SIGNED BY THE PHYSICIAN STATING THE CONDITION AND ANY TREATMENT IMPLEMENTED. THIS LETTER SHOULD ALSO INDICATE WHETHER THIS CONDITION OR TREATMENT WILL HAVE ANY EFFECT ON THE APPLICANT’S WORK. PHYSICIAN NAME: SIGNATURE: LICENSE NUMBER: STAMP: THIS FORM MUST BE ATTESTED BY ONE OF THE TWO FOLLOWING AUTHORITIES: DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH THIS IS TO CERTIFY THAT DR. LICENSE NUMBER: i (2) IS CURRENTLY LICENSED TO PRACTICE MEDICINE. qd) AUTHORIZED SIGNATURE : STAMP OR SEAL OF THE STATE AUTHORITY (COLLEGE OF PHYSICIANS) SUBMIT TO THE CONSULAR SECTION THREE ORIGINALS COPIES OF THIS MEDICAL REPORT AND TWO COPIES OF ALL RESULTS OF THE MEDICAL TESTS. DO NOT SUBMIT X-RAYS AS THOSE MUST BE PRESENTED TO THE HEALTH AUTHORITIES IN SAUDI ARABIA ALONG WITH ONE CLEAR COPY OF THIS REPORT AND ALL TEST RESULTS.'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7511887974672512}, {''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': "# Nutrition and Healthy Eating Guide ## Macronutrients ### Carbohydrates Carbohydrates are the body''s main source of energy. They are found in foods like fruits, vegetables, grains, and legumes. - Recommended intake: 45-65% of total daily calories - Complex carbohydrates (whole grains, vegetables) are preferred over simple sugars - Dietary fiber (25-30 grams per day) is essential for digestive health - Limit added sugars to less than 10% of daily calories ### Proteins Proteins are essential for building and repairing tissues, making enzymes and hormones, and supporting immune function. - Recommended intake: 10-35% of total daily calories - Good sources: lean meats, fish, eggs, dairy, beans, nuts, tofu - Complete proteins contain all essential amino acids - Plant-based proteins (beans, lentils, nuts) are heart-healthy options ### Fats Fats are necessary for nutrient absorption, hormone production, and brain health. - Recommended intake: 20-35% of total daily calories - Unsaturated fats (olive oil, avocados, nuts) are healthier choices - Limit saturated fats to less than 10% of daily calories - Avoid trans fats completely - Omega-3 fatty acids (fish, flaxseed, walnuts) support heart health ## Micronutrients ### Essential Vitamins - Vitamin A: Vision, immune function (carrots, sweet potatoes, spinach) - Vitamin B12: Nerve function, red blood cells (meat, fish, dairy) - Vitamin C: Immune function, skin health (citrus fruits, peppers, berries) - Vitamin D: Bone health, immune function (sunlight, fatty fish, fortified foods) - Vitamin K: Blood clotting, bone health (leafy greens, broccoli) ### Essential Minerals - Calcium: Bone health (dairy, leafy greens, fortified foods) - Iron: Oxygen transport (red meat, spinach, beans) - Potassium: Heart function, blood pressure (bananas, potatoes, avocados) - Zinc: Immune function, wound healing (meat, seeds, legumes) ## Hydration ### Water Intake - General recommendation: 8-10 glasses (2-2.5 liters) per day - Needs increase with exercise, heat, illness, or pregnancy - Signs of dehydration: dark urine, dry mouth, fatigue, headache - Water is the best choice for hydration - Limit sugary drinks and excessive caffeine ### Beverages to Limit - Sugary sodas and energy drinks - Fruit juices with added sugar - Excessive alcohol (1 drink/day women, 2 drinks/day men) - High-caffeine beverages ## Healthy Eating Patterns ### Mediterranean Diet - Rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts - Olive oil as primary fat source - Moderate fish and poultry - Limited red meat - Associated with reduced heart disease, diabetes, and cancer risk ### DASH Diet - Designed to lower blood pressure - High in fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Low in sodium, red meat, added sugars - Rich in potassium, calcium, magnesium ## Dietary Tips for Specific Conditions ### For Diabetes - Monitor carbohydrate intake - Choose low glycemic index foods - Eat regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat", ''relevance_score'': 0.6690970834076518}]','2026-07-29 07:16:19.258628');
