INSERT INTO messages VALUES(66,14,'assistant',unistr('Hello! How can I assist you with your health or medical questions today?\u000a\u000a**Reminder:** I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional for any health concerns.'),'[{''title'': ''nutrition_guide'', ''content'': "# Nutrition and Healthy Eating Guide ## Macronutrients ### Carbohydrates Carbohydrates are the body''s main source of energy. They are found in foods like fruits, vegetables, grains, and legumes. - Recommended intake: 45-65% of total daily calories - Complex carbohydrates (whole grains, vegetables) are preferred over simple sugars - Dietary fiber (25-30 grams per day) is essential for digestive health - Limit added sugars to less than 10% of daily calories ### Proteins Proteins are essential for building and repairing tissues, making enzymes and hormones, and supporting immune function. - Recommended intake: 10-35% of total daily calories - Good sources: lean meats, fish, eggs, dairy, beans, nuts, tofu - Complete proteins contain all essential amino acids - Plant-based proteins (beans, lentils, nuts) are heart-healthy options ### Fats Fats are necessary for nutrient absorption, hormone production, and brain health. - Recommended intake: 20-35% of total daily calories - Unsaturated fats (olive oil, avocados, nuts) are healthier choices - Limit saturated fats to less than 10% of daily calories - Avoid trans fats completely - Omega-3 fatty acids (fish, flaxseed, walnuts) support heart health ## Micronutrients ### Essential Vitamins - Vitamin A: Vision, immune function (carrots, sweet potatoes, spinach) - Vitamin B12: Nerve function, red blood cells (meat, fish, dairy) - Vitamin C: Immune function, skin health (citrus fruits, peppers, berries) - Vitamin D: Bone health, immune function (sunlight, fatty fish, fortified foods) - Vitamin K: Blood clotting, bone health (leafy greens, broccoli) ### Essential Minerals - Calcium: Bone health (dairy, leafy greens, fortified foods) - Iron: Oxygen transport (red meat, spinach, beans) - Potassium: Heart function, blood pressure (bananas, potatoes, avocados) - Zinc: Immune function, wound healing (meat, seeds, legumes) ## Hydration ### Water Intake - General recommendation: 8-10 glasses (2-2.5 liters) per day - Needs increase with exercise, heat, illness, or pregnancy - Signs of dehydration: dark urine, dry mouth, fatigue, headache - Water is the best choice for hydration - Limit sugary drinks and excessive caffeine ### Beverages to Limit - Sugary sodas and energy drinks - Fruit juices with added sugar - Excessive alcohol (1 drink/day women, 2 drinks/day men) - High-caffeine beverages ## Healthy Eating Patterns ### Mediterranean Diet - Rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts - Olive oil as primary fat source - Moderate fish and poultry - Limited red meat - Associated with reduced heart disease, diabetes, and cancer risk ### DASH Diet - Designed to lower blood pressure - High in fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Low in sodium, red meat, added sugars - Rich in potassium, calcium, magnesium ## Dietary Tips for Specific Conditions ### For Diabetes - Monitor carbohydrate intake - Choose low glycemic index foods - Eat regular meals to maintain blood sugar - Include fiber-rich foods - Limit refined sugars and processed foods ### For Heart Health - Reduce sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Increase omega-3 fatty acids - Limit saturated and trans fats - Eat plenty of fruits and vegetables - Choose whole grains over refined grains ### For Weight Management - Control portion sizes - Eat", ''relevance_score'': 0.64623112178084}, {''title'': ''WhatsApp Image 2026-07-29 at 12.44.30 (2)'', ''content'': ''m R SCANS MR Hospitals No.1, Yagappa Nagar, Thanjavur - 613 007. Tamil Nadu, S.tndia Ph ; 04362 - 278004, 278005, 278006, 278007, 278008, 279007, 237228, website ; www.mrhospitals.com e-mail ; mrhospitals_tnj@yahoo.in iname |MR.XAVIER Reg No: 35264 aes | 88 Y/M Report Date | 96,01.2026 bali cr Ref: Dr. DR.MLMATHEWS.MS.MCH., tee Jae CECT- CHEST Helical contrast study of the chest: The trachea and major bronchi are normal. The thoracic aorta and its branches are normal. es are normal. cava are normal. The main pulmonary artery and its branch The right and left brachiocephalic trunks and the superior vena Multiple pneumatocele is seen involving right upper and left lower lobe, largest 5 approximately measuring around 14 x 13 mm. ‘Associated mild surrounding ground- glass opacities and fine nodules. : The parenchyma of rest of lungs shows no significant abnormality. No pleural thickening /nodularity / effusion is present. I lymphadenopathy is present. wall thickening noted involving distal No significant hilar / mediastinal Mild diffuse circumferential mildly enhancing thickness measuring around 10 oesophagus extending into OG junction, maximum mm. Rest of thoracic esophagus is normal. The thoracic cage is normal. IMPRESSION: Multiple pneumatocele is seen involving right upper and left lower lobe. Associated mild surrounding ground-glass opaciti ssocie f pacities and fine nodules Mild diffuse circumferential mildl i i i j V i ly enhancing wall thickening noted i i distal oesophagus extending into OG junction — Needs pind and ps Dr. Prasanna K , MBBS, MDRE Consultant Radiologist Reg. No. : TNMC92729 Pp'', ''relevance_score'': 0.642569662856037}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6423369254639889}, {''title'': ''hypertension'', ''content'': ''# Hypertension (High Blood Pressure) ## What is Hypertension? Hypertension, also known as high blood pressure, is a long-term medical condition in which the blood pressure in the arteries is persistently elevated. High blood pressure typically does not cause symptoms. Long-term high blood pressure, however, is a major risk factor for stroke, heart attack, heart failure, peripheral arterial disease, vision loss, chronic kidney disease, and dementia. ## Blood Pressure Categories ### Normal Blood Pressure - Systolic: less than 120 mmHg - Diastolic: less than 80 mmHg ### Elevated Blood Pressure - Systolic: 120-129 mmHg - Diastolic: less than 80 mmHg ### High Blood Pressure Stage 1 - Systolic: 130-139 mmHg - Diastolic: 80-89 mmHg ### High Blood Pressure Stage 2 - Systolic: 140 or higher mmHg - Diastolic: 90 or higher mmHg ### Hypertensive Crisis - Systolic: higher than 180 mmHg - Diastolic: higher than 120 mmHg ## Symptoms Hypertension is often called the "silent killer" because it usually has no symptoms. Some people with very high blood pressure may experience: - Headaches - Shortness of breath - Nosebleeds - Dizziness - Chest pain - Visual changes - Blood in urine ## Risk Factors - Age (risk increases with age) - Family history of hypertension - Being overweight or obese - Lack of physical activity - High sodium diet - Low potassium diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Stress - Chronic kidney disease - Sleep apnea - Certain medications (NSAIDs, birth control pills, decongestants) ## Management ### Lifestyle Modifications - DASH diet (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) - Reduce sodium intake to less than 2,300 mg per day - Regular aerobic exercise (30 minutes most days) - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Limit alcohol to 1 drink per day for women, 2 for men - Quit smoking - Manage stress through relaxation techniques - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) ### Medications - ACE inhibitors (lisinopril, enalapril) - ARBs (losartan, valsartan) - Calcium channel blockers (amlodipine, nifedipine) - Thiazide diuretics (hydrochlorothiazide) - Beta-blockers (metoprolol, atenolol) ## Complications of Untreated Hypertension - Heart attack or heart failure - Stroke - Kidney damage or failure - Vision loss - Peripheral artery disease - Vascular dementia - Hypertensive emergency ## When to Seek Medical Attention If your blood pressure reading is 180/120 mmHg or higher, wait five minutes and test again. If it is still elevated, contact your doctor immediately. A blood pressure reading this high can lead to a stroke or organ damage.'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6415854867071192}, {''title'': ''Medical-report-1-2048'', ''content'': ''a MEDICAL REPORT = (2 8 cigs NAME: NATIONALITY: | SEX: | AGE: MARITAL STATUS: PASSPORT NO: | ISSUE PLACE: | ISSUE DATE: POSITION APPLIED FOR: PHOTO DEAR SIR/ MADAM PLEASE, ARRANGE TO EXAMINE THE ABOVE MENTIONED CANDIDATE AS TO HIS/HER FITNESS FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED POSITION. DATE / / RECRUITMENT ATTACHE/OR DOCTOR: HISTORY OF ANY SIGNIFICANT PAST ILLNESS INCLUDING: - PSYCHIATRIC AND NEUROLOGICAL DISORDERS (EPILEPSY, DEPRESSION...) - ALLERGY MEDICAL EXAMINATION LABORATORY INVESTIGATION TYPE OF MEDICAL EXAMINATION NEGATIVE\\ POSITIVE\\ TYPE OF LABORATORY NEGATIVE\\ POSITIVE\\ NORMAL ABNORMAL INVESTIGATION NORMAL ABNORMAL VISION R. EYE (URINE) L. EYE - SUGAR EYE - ALBUMIN OTHER R. EYE - BILHARZIASIS L. EYE - OTHER EAR R. EAR (STOOL) - HELMINTHES CHEST X - RAY - SALMONELLA/SHIGELLA PULMONARY TUBERCULOSIS - V.CHOLERA (SYSTEMIC EXAMINATION) - OTHER BLOOD PRESSURE (BLOOD) HEART - HEMOGLOBIN LUNGS - MALARIA FILM ABDOMEN - OTHERS (OTHERS) (SEROLOGY) *HERNIA - HIV TEST *VARICOSE VEINS EXTREMITIES -F.B.S. SKIN - HBSAG/ANTI HCV (VENEREAL DISEASES VDRL TPHA PREGNANCY TEST CONFIRM IF THE APPLICATION HAS ONE OF THE FOLLOWING: NO YES COMMUNICABLE DISEASES MENTAL DISORDER MENTAL RETARDATION PHYSICAL DISORDERS HANDICAP PARALYSIS BLINDNESS HEARING DISORDER SPEECH DISORDER MENTIONED ABOVE IS THE MEDICAL REPORT FOR MR/ MRS/ MISS » WHOIS [ ] FIT [ ] UNFIT FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED JOB. -TO BE FIT, ALL MEDICAL EXAMINATIONS AND LABORATORY INVESTIGATIONS MUST BE WITHIN NORMAL LIMITS. IN THE EVENT OF AN ABNORMAL/POSITIVE RESULT, A TYPEWRITTEN LETTER SIGNED BY THE PHYSICIAN STATING THE CONDITION AND ANY TREATMENT IMPLEMENTED. THIS LETTER SHOULD ALSO INDICATE WHETHER THIS CONDITION OR TREATMENT WILL HAVE ANY EFFECT ON THE APPLICANT’S WORK. PHYSICIAN NAME: SIGNATURE: LICENSE NUMBER: STAMP: THIS FORM MUST BE ATTESTED BY ONE OF THE TWO FOLLOWING AUTHORITIES: DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH THIS IS TO CERTIFY THAT DR. LICENSE NUMBER: i (2) IS CURRENTLY LICENSED TO PRACTICE MEDICINE. qd) AUTHORIZED SIGNATURE : STAMP OR SEAL OF THE STATE AUTHORITY (COLLEGE OF PHYSICIANS) SUBMIT TO THE CONSULAR SECTION THREE ORIGINALS COPIES OF THIS MEDICAL REPORT AND TWO COPIES OF ALL RESULTS OF THE MEDICAL TESTS. DO NOT SUBMIT X-RAYS AS THOSE MUST BE PRESENTED TO THE HEALTH AUTHORITIES IN SAUDI ARABIA ALONG WITH ONE CLEAR COPY OF THIS REPORT AND ALL TEST RESULTS.'', ''relevance_score'': 0.63838149648275}]','2026-07-29 07:20:37.434320');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(67,14,'user','which is the best hospital for heart issue in India and who is the best doctor','','2026-07-29 07:21:16.401522');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(68,14,'assistant',unistr('India has several world-class hospitals and renowned cardiologists specializing in heart care. Here are some of the **top hospitals for heart treatment in India**, along with some of the **leading cardiologists** associated with them:\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Top Hospitals for Heart Care in India**\u000a#### **1. All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), New Delhi**\u000a   - **Why?** One of India’s most prestigious public hospitals, offering advanced cardiac care, research, and affordable treatment.\u000a   - **Specialties:** Coronary artery disease, heart failure, pediatric cardiology, heart transplants, and minimally invasive surgeries.\u000a   - **Notable Doctors:**\u000a     - **Dr. Balram Bhargava** (Former Director-General, ICMR; Cardiologist)\u000a     - **Dr. Sandeep Seth** (Heart Failure & Transplant Specialist)\u000a     - **Dr. Rakesh Yadav** (Interventional Cardiology)\u000a\u000a#### **2. Fortis Escorts Heart Institute, New Delhi**\u000a   - **Why?** One of Asia’s largest dedicated cardiac care hospitals, known for high success rates in complex heart surgeries.\u000a   - **Specialties:** Coronary bypass, valve replacement, pediatric cardiology, and robotic heart surgery.\u000a   - **Notable Doctors:**\u000a     - **Dr. Ashok Seth** (Chairman, Fortis Escorts; Padma Bhushan awardee; pioneer in angioplasty)\u000a     - **Dr. T.S. Kler** (Electrophysiology & Arrhythmia Specialist)\u000a     - **Dr. Z.S. Meharwal** (Cardiac Surgery)\u000a\u000a#### **3. Apollo Hospitals, Chennai (and other branches)**\u000a   - **Why?** Apollo is a leader in private healthcare, with cutting-edge technology and a strong cardiac unit.\u000a   - **Specialties:** Heart transplants, TAVR (Transcatheter Aortic Valve Replacement), and hybrid surgeries.\u000a   - **Notable Doctors:**\u000a     - **Dr. M.R. Girinath** (Cardiac Surgeon; Padma Shri awardee)\u000a     - **Dr. Prathap C. Reddy** (Founder, Apollo Hospitals; Cardiologist)\u000a     - **Dr. Sai Satish** (Interventional Cardiology)\u000a\u000a#### **4. Medanta – The Medicity, Gurgaon**\u000a   - **Why?** A multi-specialty hospital with a dedicated **Heart Institute** led by Dr. Naresh Trehan.\u000a   - **Specialties:** Minimally invasive heart surgery, heart transplants, and advanced electrophysiology.\u000a   - **Notable Doctors:**\u000a     - **Dr. Naresh Trehan** (Chairman, Medanta; Padma Bhushan & Padma Shri awardee; Cardiac Surgeon)\u000a     - **Dr. Praveen Chandra** (Interventional Cardiology; Padma Shri awardee)\u000a     - **Dr. Rajneesh Malhotra** (Cardiac Surgery)\u000a\u000a#### **5. Narayana Health (NH), Bangalore & Kolkata**\u000a   - **Why?** Known for **affordable yet high-quality** cardiac care, with a high volume of heart surgeries.\u000a   - **Specialties:** Pediatric heart surgery, coronary bypass, and valve repairs.\u000a   - **Notable Doctors:**\u000a     - **Dr. Devi Shetty** (Founder, Narayana Health; Padma Bhushan awardee; pioneer in low-cost heart surgery)\u000a     - **Dr. Julius Punnen** (Cardiac Surgery)\u000a\u000a#### **6. Max Super Speciality Hospital, Delhi & Saket**\u000a   - **Why?** Advanced cardiac care with a focus on **minimally invasive procedures** and robotic surgery.\u000a   - **Specialties:** Heart failure management, TAVR, and electrophysiology.\u000a   - **Notable Doctors:**\u000a     - **Dr. Kewal Krishan** (Heart Transplant & LVAD Specialist)\u000a     - **Dr. Vanita Arora** (Electrophysiology & Arrhythmia Specialist)\u000a\u000a#### **7. Kokilaben Dhirubhai Ambani Hospital, Mumbai**\u000a   - **Why?** One of Mumbai’s best for **complex heart surgeries** and pediatric cardiology.\u000a   - **Specialties:** Heart transplants, congenital heart disease, and hybrid procedures.\u000a   - **Notable Doctors:**\u000a     - **Dr. Nandkishore Kapadia** (Cardiac Surgery)\u000a     - **Dr. Prafulla Kerkar** (Interventional Cardiology)\u000a\u000a#### **8. Sri Jayadeva Institute of Cardiovascular Sciences, Bangalore**\u000a   - **Why?** A **government-run** institute with **high success rates** in cardiac surgeries at **low cost**.\u000a   - **Specialties:** Pediatric heart surgery, coronary interventions, and heart failure management.\u000a   - **Notable Doctors:**\u000a     - **Dr. C.N.'),'[{''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "# Heart Disease - Overview ## What is Heart Disease? Heart disease, also known as cardiovascular disease, refers to conditions that involve narrowed or blocked blood vessels that can lead to a heart attack, chest pain (angina), or stroke. It is the leading cause of death worldwide. Common heart diseases include coronary artery disease, heart failure, arrhythmias, and valve problems. ## Types of Heart Disease ### Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) The most common type of heart disease. CAD occurs when the coronary arteries become narrowed or blocked due to plaque buildup (atherosclerosis). This reduces blood flow to the heart muscle and can lead to heart attack. ### Heart Failure Heart failure means the heart cannot pump blood as well as it should. Despite the name, it does not mean the heart has stopped working. It means the heart is not pumping as well as the body needs. ### Arrhythmias Arrhythmias are irregular heartbeats. The heart may beat too quickly (tachycardia), too slowly (bradycardia), or with an irregular pattern. ### Heart Valve Disease When one or more heart valves do not work properly, the heart must work harder to pump blood through the body. ## Risk Factors ### Non-Modifiable Risk Factors - Age (men over 45, women over 55) - Gender (men at higher risk at younger ages) - Family history of heart disease - Race/ethnicity (higher risk for certain populations) ### Modifiable Risk Factors - High blood pressure (hypertension) - High cholesterol - Diabetes - Obesity (BMI > 30) - Smoking - Physical inactivity - Unhealthy diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Chronic stress ## Warning Signs ### Heart Attack Symptoms - Chest pain or discomfort (pressure, squeezing, fullness) - Pain in one or both arms, the back, neck, jaw, or stomach - Shortness of breath - Cold sweat, nausea, lightheadedness - Women may experience atypical symptoms: unusual fatigue, indigestion-like discomfort ### Heart Failure Symptoms - Shortness of breath during activity or when lying down - Fatigue and weakness - Swelling in legs, ankles, and feet - Rapid or irregular heartbeat - Reduced ability to exercise - Persistent cough or wheezing - Swelling of abdomen - Rapid weight gain from fluid retention ## Diagnosis - Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) - Echocardiogram - Stress test - Cardiac catheterization - Blood tests (troponin, BNP, cholesterol) - Coronary angiogram - CT scan or MRI ## Prevention ### Diet - Heart-healthy diet (DASH or Mediterranean) - Limit saturated fat to less than 10% of calories - Limit sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Eat plenty of fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Choose lean proteins - Include omega-3 rich foods ### Exercise - At least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week - Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs", ''relevance_score'': 0.7543274124483371}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.7449410985443173}, {''title'': ''WhatsApp Image 2026-07-29 at 12.44.30 (2)'', ''content'': ''m R SCANS MR Hospitals No.1, Yagappa Nagar, Thanjavur - 613 007. Tamil Nadu, S.tndia Ph ; 04362 - 278004, 278005, 278006, 278007, 278008, 279007, 237228, website ; www.mrhospitals.com e-mail ; mrhospitals_tnj@yahoo.in iname |MR.XAVIER Reg No: 35264 aes | 88 Y/M Report Date | 96,01.2026 bali cr Ref: Dr. DR.MLMATHEWS.MS.MCH., tee Jae CECT- CHEST Helical contrast study of the chest: The trachea and major bronchi are normal. The thoracic aorta and its branches are normal. es are normal. cava are normal. The main pulmonary artery and its branch The right and left brachiocephalic trunks and the superior vena Multiple pneumatocele is seen involving right upper and left lower lobe, largest 5 approximately measuring around 14 x 13 mm. ‘Associated mild surrounding ground- glass opacities and fine nodules. : The parenchyma of rest of lungs shows no significant abnormality. No pleural thickening /nodularity / effusion is present. I lymphadenopathy is present. wall thickening noted involving distal No significant hilar / mediastinal Mild diffuse circumferential mildly enhancing thickness measuring around 10 oesophagus extending into OG junction, maximum mm. Rest of thoracic esophagus is normal. The thoracic cage is normal. IMPRESSION: Multiple pneumatocele is seen involving right upper and left lower lobe. Associated mild surrounding ground-glass opaciti ssocie f pacities and fine nodules Mild diffuse circumferential mildl i i i j V i ly enhancing wall thickening noted i i distal oesophagus extending into OG junction — Needs pind and ps Dr. Prasanna K , MBBS, MDRE Consultant Radiologist Reg. No. : TNMC92729 Pp'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7377821574007788}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.6979223044242001}, {''title'': ''Medical-report-1-2048'', ''content'': ''a MEDICAL REPORT = (2 8 cigs NAME: NATIONALITY: | SEX: | AGE: MARITAL STATUS: PASSPORT NO: | ISSUE PLACE: | ISSUE DATE: POSITION APPLIED FOR: PHOTO DEAR SIR/ MADAM PLEASE, ARRANGE TO EXAMINE THE ABOVE MENTIONED CANDIDATE AS TO HIS/HER FITNESS FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED POSITION. DATE / / RECRUITMENT ATTACHE/OR DOCTOR: HISTORY OF ANY SIGNIFICANT PAST ILLNESS INCLUDING: - PSYCHIATRIC AND NEUROLOGICAL DISORDERS (EPILEPSY, DEPRESSION...) - ALLERGY MEDICAL EXAMINATION LABORATORY INVESTIGATION TYPE OF MEDICAL EXAMINATION NEGATIVE\\ POSITIVE\\ TYPE OF LABORATORY NEGATIVE\\ POSITIVE\\ NORMAL ABNORMAL INVESTIGATION NORMAL ABNORMAL VISION R. EYE (URINE) L. EYE - SUGAR EYE - ALBUMIN OTHER R. EYE - BILHARZIASIS L. EYE - OTHER EAR R. EAR (STOOL) - HELMINTHES CHEST X - RAY - SALMONELLA/SHIGELLA PULMONARY TUBERCULOSIS - V.CHOLERA (SYSTEMIC EXAMINATION) - OTHER BLOOD PRESSURE (BLOOD) HEART - HEMOGLOBIN LUNGS - MALARIA FILM ABDOMEN - OTHERS (OTHERS) (SEROLOGY) *HERNIA - HIV TEST *VARICOSE VEINS EXTREMITIES -F.B.S. SKIN - HBSAG/ANTI HCV (VENEREAL DISEASES VDRL TPHA PREGNANCY TEST CONFIRM IF THE APPLICATION HAS ONE OF THE FOLLOWING: NO YES COMMUNICABLE DISEASES MENTAL DISORDER MENTAL RETARDATION PHYSICAL DISORDERS HANDICAP PARALYSIS BLINDNESS HEARING DISORDER SPEECH DISORDER MENTIONED ABOVE IS THE MEDICAL REPORT FOR MR/ MRS/ MISS » WHOIS [ ] FIT [ ] UNFIT FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED JOB. -TO BE FIT, ALL MEDICAL EXAMINATIONS AND LABORATORY INVESTIGATIONS MUST BE WITHIN NORMAL LIMITS. IN THE EVENT OF AN ABNORMAL/POSITIVE RESULT, A TYPEWRITTEN LETTER SIGNED BY THE PHYSICIAN STATING THE CONDITION AND ANY TREATMENT IMPLEMENTED. THIS LETTER SHOULD ALSO INDICATE WHETHER THIS CONDITION OR TREATMENT WILL HAVE ANY EFFECT ON THE APPLICANT’S WORK. PHYSICIAN NAME: SIGNATURE: LICENSE NUMBER: STAMP: THIS FORM MUST BE ATTESTED BY ONE OF THE TWO FOLLOWING AUTHORITIES: DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH THIS IS TO CERTIFY THAT DR. LICENSE NUMBER: i (2) IS CURRENTLY LICENSED TO PRACTICE MEDICINE. qd) AUTHORIZED SIGNATURE : STAMP OR SEAL OF THE STATE AUTHORITY (COLLEGE OF PHYSICIANS) SUBMIT TO THE CONSULAR SECTION THREE ORIGINALS COPIES OF THIS MEDICAL REPORT AND TWO COPIES OF ALL RESULTS OF THE MEDICAL TESTS. DO NOT SUBMIT X-RAYS AS THOSE MUST BE PRESENTED TO THE HEALTH AUTHORITIES IN SAUDI ARABIA ALONG WITH ONE CLEAR COPY OF THIS REPORT AND ALL TEST RESULTS.'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6919515741603183}]','2026-07-29 07:21:33.755039');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(69,15,'user','what are they telling in this report.','','2026-07-29 07:57:51.270080');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(70,15,'assistant',unistr('This medical report is a **pre-employment health screening form**, typically required for job applicants (often for work visas or overseas employment, such as in Saudi Arabia). Here’s what it covers:\u000a\u000a1. **Purpose**:\u000a   - To assess whether the candidate is **medically fit** for the job they’ve applied for.\u000a   - All tests must be **normal/negative** for the candidate to be declared "FIT." Any abnormal results require a detailed physician’s letter explaining the condition and its impact on work.\u000a\u000a2. **Key Sections**:\u000a   - **Personal Details**: Name, age, sex, nationality, passport info, and job position.\u000a   - **Medical History**: Past illnesses (e.g., psychiatric disorders, epilepsy, allergies, depression).\u000a   - **Physical Examination**: Vision, ears, blood pressure, heart, lungs, abdomen, skin, extremities (e.g., hernia, varicose veins).\u000a   - **Laboratory Tests**:\u000a     - **Urine**: Sugar, albumin, bilharzia (parasitic infection).\u000a     - **Stool**: Helminths (worms), salmonella/shigella (bacterial infections), cholera.\u000a     - **Blood**: Hemoglobin, malaria, fasting blood sugar (FBS), HIV, hepatitis B/C (HBsAg/Anti-HCV), syphilis (VDRL/TPHA).\u000a     - **Chest X-ray**: For tuberculosis (TB) or other lung conditions.\u000a     - **Pregnancy Test** (if applicable).\u000a   - **Disabilities/Disorders**: Checks for communicable diseases, mental/physical disabilities, blindness, deafness, paralysis, etc.\u000a\u000a3. **Outcome**:\u000a   - The physician marks the candidate as **"FIT"** or **"UNFIT"** based on test results.\u000a   - If **unfit**, a letter from the doctor must explain the condition, treatment, and whether it affects work ability.\u000a\u000a4. **Submission**:\u000a   - The report must be **attested** by a health authority or medical board.\u000a   - Three original copies + test results are submitted to the consular section (X-rays are submitted separately in Saudi Arabia).\u000a\u000a**Note**: This is a **standardized form** used to ensure applicants meet health requirements for employment, especially in roles where public health or safety is a concern.\u000a\u000a---\u000a*I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional.*'),'[{''title'': ''Medical-report-1-2048'', ''content'': ''a MEDICAL REPORT = (2 8 cigs NAME: NATIONALITY: | SEX: | AGE: MARITAL STATUS: PASSPORT NO: | ISSUE PLACE: | ISSUE DATE: POSITION APPLIED FOR: PHOTO DEAR SIR/ MADAM PLEASE, ARRANGE TO EXAMINE THE ABOVE MENTIONED CANDIDATE AS TO HIS/HER FITNESS FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED POSITION. DATE / / RECRUITMENT ATTACHE/OR DOCTOR: HISTORY OF ANY SIGNIFICANT PAST ILLNESS INCLUDING: - PSYCHIATRIC AND NEUROLOGICAL DISORDERS (EPILEPSY, DEPRESSION...) - ALLERGY MEDICAL EXAMINATION LABORATORY INVESTIGATION TYPE OF MEDICAL EXAMINATION NEGATIVE\\ POSITIVE\\ TYPE OF LABORATORY NEGATIVE\\ POSITIVE\\ NORMAL ABNORMAL INVESTIGATION NORMAL ABNORMAL VISION R. EYE (URINE) L. EYE - SUGAR EYE - ALBUMIN OTHER R. EYE - BILHARZIASIS L. EYE - OTHER EAR R. EAR (STOOL) - HELMINTHES CHEST X - RAY - SALMONELLA/SHIGELLA PULMONARY TUBERCULOSIS - V.CHOLERA (SYSTEMIC EXAMINATION) - OTHER BLOOD PRESSURE (BLOOD) HEART - HEMOGLOBIN LUNGS - MALARIA FILM ABDOMEN - OTHERS (OTHERS) (SEROLOGY) *HERNIA - HIV TEST *VARICOSE VEINS EXTREMITIES -F.B.S. SKIN - HBSAG/ANTI HCV (VENEREAL DISEASES VDRL TPHA PREGNANCY TEST CONFIRM IF THE APPLICATION HAS ONE OF THE FOLLOWING: NO YES COMMUNICABLE DISEASES MENTAL DISORDER MENTAL RETARDATION PHYSICAL DISORDERS HANDICAP PARALYSIS BLINDNESS HEARING DISORDER SPEECH DISORDER MENTIONED ABOVE IS THE MEDICAL REPORT FOR MR/ MRS/ MISS » WHOIS [ ] FIT [ ] UNFIT FOR THE ABOVE MENTIONED JOB. -TO BE FIT, ALL MEDICAL EXAMINATIONS AND LABORATORY INVESTIGATIONS MUST BE WITHIN NORMAL LIMITS. IN THE EVENT OF AN ABNORMAL/POSITIVE RESULT, A TYPEWRITTEN LETTER SIGNED 