INSERT INTO messages VALUES(71,15,'user','why do we use paracetamol tablet for?','','2026-07-29 08:01:38.074507');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(72,15,'assistant',unistr('Paracetamol (also known as acetaminophen) is a commonly used **over-the-counter medication** with the following key uses:\u000a\u000a1. **Pain Relief** – Helps reduce mild to moderate pain, such as headaches, muscle aches, toothaches, menstrual cramps, and arthritis discomfort.\u000a2. **Fever Reduction** – Lowers high body temperature (fever) caused by infections like colds, flu, or other illnesses.\u000a3. **Safe for Most People** – Unlike NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen), it is gentler on the stomach and does not increase bleeding risk, making it suitable for children, pregnant women (under medical guidance), and those with sensitive stomachs.\u000a\u000a**Important Notes:**\u000a- **Dosage**: Follow the recommended dose (typically 500–1000 mg every 4–6 hours, max 4000 mg/day for adults). Overdosing can cause **liver damage**.\u000a- **Not for Inflammation**: Unlike ibuprofen, paracetamol does **not** reduce inflammation.\u000a- **Consult a Doctor** if symptoms persist beyond 3 days, if you have liver disease, or if taking other medications.\u000a\u000a---\u000a*I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional.*'),'[{''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6718331207721973}, {''title'': ''images-2'', ''content'': ''Patient Information Form Name: Date: Wendi your problem stat?; __escibe Probe Cause of Current Problem: (D caracdent Cl Woskinuy CO Gaduatonset other Did this Problem require Surgery: No Cl Yes 0 YesDateot surgery ast Medical History Oo youhaveaisry fhe fosoning problems? 2 Breathing Pblems D Seok 2 Depression Pregnant 1D BonajeintPeblems Bowed C1 Heat mebiems 1D Kidney Prabiems Wir then aol we G CanentWoundskinPrebems ©) Galbaddener Og ute Pacemaker 1 ectrclimpant © smoing 1 Tmoucancer sity atch: Headaches C Oisbete 1D Seep Apnea 1D NoSurgeries SugeniesMospeaations ver Complains 1D NoMedicaton Medications Peselt Medations that you ae taking eda) Dove FawsonorMeseaton TT No Keown allergies Allergies latex Ove Ome Todine Ove Ne Bromine Ws Cl no other Do you have anyreigiousicltrlvews that wil affect your treatment?” No Over ‘tional commentieading or Memory Problem) Signature bate'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6694773239314286}, {''title'': ''images-2'', ''content'': ''Patient Information Form Name: Date: Wendi your problem stat?; __escibe Probe Cause of Current Problem: (D caracdent Cl Woskinuy CO Gaduatonset other Did this Problem require Surgery: No Cl Yes 0 YesDateot surgery ast Medical History Oo youhaveaisry fhe fosoning problems? 2 Breathing Pblems D Seok 2 Depression Pregnant 1D BonajeintPeblems Bowed C1 Heat mebiems 1D Kidney Prabiems Wir then aol we G CanentWoundskinPrebems ©) Galbaddener Og ute Pacemaker 1 ectrclimpant © smoing 1 Tmoucancer sity atch: Headaches C Oisbete 1D Seep Apnea 1D NoSurgeries SugeniesMospeaations ver Complains 1D NoMedicaton Medications Peselt Medations that you ae taking eda) Dove FawsonorMeseaton TT No Keown allergies Allergies latex Ove Ome Todine Ove Ne Bromine Ws Cl no other Do you have anyreigiousicltrlvews that wil affect your treatment?” No Over ‘tional commentieading or Memory Problem) Signature bate'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6694773239314286}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.662287493002509}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6615384771233699}]','2026-07-29 08:01:43.716321');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(73,16,'user','suggest me some tablets for my body pain and headache.','','2026-07-29 08:16:31.420333');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(74,16,'assistant',unistr('I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional.\u000a\u000aFor general body pain and headaches, common over-the-counter options include:\u000a- **Acetaminophen (Paracetamol)** – Effective for pain and fever, but avoid if you have liver issues.\u000a- **Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)** like **Ibuprofen** or **Naproxen** – Help with pain and inflammation but may irritate the stomach or affect kidneys.\u000a\u000a**Important:**\u000a- Follow dosage instructions carefully.\u000a- Avoid mixing medications without medical advice.\u000a- If pain persists or worsens, consult a doctor to rule out underlying conditions.\u000a\u000aWould you like guidance on non-medication pain relief methods as well?'),'[{''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6985320091689813}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6914092031880971}]','2026-07-29 08:16:35.851493');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(75,16,'user','what is the use of paracetamol table and what are their side effects?','','2026-07-29 08:17:13.675023');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(76,16,'assistant',unistr('I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional.\u000a\u000a**Paracetamol (also called acetaminophen)** is a commonly used medication for:\u000a- Relieving mild to moderate pain (headaches, muscle aches, toothaches, menstrual cramps).\u000a- Reducing fever.\u000a\u000a### **Common Side Effects (usually mild if taken as directed):**\u000a- Nausea or stomach upset (less common than with NSAIDs like ibuprofen).\u000a- Allergic reactions (rash, itching, swelling—seek medical help if severe).\u000a- Rare but serious: **Liver damage** (if taken in high doses or with alcohol).\u000a\u000a### **Important Precautions:**\u000a- Do **not exceed the recommended dose** (typically 500–1000 mg every 4–6 hours, max 4000 mg/day for adults).\u000a- Avoid if you have **liver disease** or drink alcohol heavily.\u000a- Check with a doctor if you’re pregnant, breastfeeding, or taking other medications.\u000a\u000aWould you like guidance on safe dosing or alternatives?'),'[{''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6794988129088358}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6624016800082408}]','2026-07-29 08:17:19.490352');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(77,16,'user','uses of ibuprofen','','2026-07-29 08:18:23.471273');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(78,16,'assistant',unistr('I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional.\u000a\u000a**Ibuprofen** is a **nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID)** commonly used for:\u000a\u000a### **Primary Uses:**\u000a1. **Pain Relief** – Headaches, muscle pain, dental pain, menstrual cramps, arthritis, and minor injuries.\u000a2. **Reducing Inflammation** – Swelling from sprains, strains, or chronic conditions like rheumatoid arthritis.\u000a3. **Fever Reduction** – Helps lower high body temperature.\u000a4. **Post-surgical or injury pain** – Often prescribed for short-term pain management.\u000a\u000a### **How It Works:**\u000a- Blocks **prostaglandins** (chemicals in the body that cause pain, inflammation, and fever).\u000a\u000aWould you like details on dosage, precautions, or side effects?'),'[{''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6651584935290819}, {''title'': ''fever_cold_flu'', ''content'': ''Symptoms that improve but then return with fever and worse cough ### Flu Prevention - Annual flu vaccination (best protection) - Frequent hand washing - Avoid close contact with sick people - Cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing - Disinfect frequently touched surfaces - Stay home when sick ### Flu Treatment - Antiviral medications (oseltamivir, zanamivir) if started within 48 hours - Rest - Fluids - Over-the-counter medications for fever and symptoms'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6506868516339818}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.6453396599417653}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.6258996886748152}, {''title'': ''1mg: Ibuprofen'', ''url'': ''https://www.1mg.com/generics/ibuprofen-210020'', ''content'': ''Uses: Ibuprofen is used for pain relief and fever . It relieves pain and inflammation in conditions like headache, muscle pain, dental pain or joint pain. | Side Effects: Most side effects do not require any medical attention and disappear as your body adjusts to the medicine. Consult your doctor if they persist or if you’re worried about them Vomiting, Nausea, Dizziness, Abdominal pain, Constipation, Flatulence, Diarrhea, Dyspepsia, Headache, Fatigue, Rash'', ''relevance_score'': 0.95}]','2026-07-29 08:18:28.187065');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(79,16,'user','Psychiatric and Anti-Anxiety Tablets.','','2026-07-29 08:19:34.485162');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(80,16,'assistant',unistr('I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional.\u000a\u000aPsychiatric and anti-anxiety medications are prescribed to manage mental health conditions like anxiety disorders, depression, insomnia, and other related issues. **These medications should only be taken under the supervision of a psychiatrist or healthcare provider.**\u000a\u000a### **Common Classes of Psychiatric & Anti-Anxiety Medications:**\u000a\u000a#### **1. Antidepressants (often used for anxiety as well)**\u000a- **Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors (SSRIs):**\u000a  - Examples: Fluoxetine, Sertraline, Escitalopram, Paroxetine.\u000a  - Uses: Depression, generalized anxiety disorder (GAD), panic disorder, social anxiety, OCD.\u000a  - Side Effects: Nausea, insomnia, weight changes, sexual dysfunction.\u000a\u000a- **Serotonin-Norepinephrine Reuptake Inhibitors (SNRIs):**\u000a  - Examples: Venlafaxine, Duloxetine.\u000a  - Uses: Depression, GAD, chronic pain (e.g., fibromyalgia).\u000a  - Side Effects: Increased blood pressure, sweating, dizziness.\u000a\u000a- **Tricyclic Antidepressants (TCAs):**\u000a  - Examples: Amitriptyline, Imipramine.\u000a  - Uses: Depression, anxiety, chronic pain, insomnia.\u000a  - Side Effects: Dry mouth, constipation, drowsiness, weight gain.\u000a\u000a- **Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs):**\u000a  - Examples: Phenelzine, Selegiline.\u000a  - Uses: Depression (especially atypical depression), anxiety.\u000a  - Side Effects: Dietary restrictions (tyramine-rich foods), interactions with other medications.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### **2. Anti-Anxiety Medications (Anxiolytics)**\u000a- **Benzodiazepines (short-term use only):**\u000a  - Examples: Alprazolam (Xanax), Diazepam (Valium), Lorazepam (Ativan), Clonazepam.\u000a  - Uses: Acute anxiety, panic attacks, insomnia, muscle spasms.\u000a  - Side Effects: Drowsiness, dizziness, dependence (risk of addiction), memory issues.\u000a  - **Note:** Benzodiazepines are typically prescribed for short-term use due to the risk of dependence.\u000a\u000a- **Buspirone:**\u000a  - Uses: Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD).\u000a  - Side Effects: Dizziness, nausea, headache.\u000a  - **Note:** Non-addictive, takes 2–4 weeks to show effects.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### **3. Mood Stabilizers**\u000a- Examples: Lithium, Valproate, Lamotrigine, Carbamazepine.\u000a- Uses: Bipolar disorder, mood swings, sometimes for depression or anxiety.\u000a- Side Effects: Weight gain, tremors, kidney/thyroid issues (with lithium), drowsiness.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### **4. Antipsychotics (for severe anxiety or mood disorders)**\u000a- Examples: Quetiapine, Aripiprazole, Olanzapine.\u000a- Uses: Schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, treatment-resistant depression, severe anxiety.\u000a- Side Effects: Weight gain, metabolic changes, drowsiness, movement disorders.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### **5. Beta-Blockers (for physical symptoms of anxiety)**\u000a- Examples: Propranolol, Atenolol.\u000a- Uses: Performance anxiety, social anxiety (reduces physical symptoms like rapid heartbeat, trembling).\u000a- Side Effects: Fatigue, dizziness, low blood pressure.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **Important Notes:**\u000a- **Never self-medicate** – These medications require a prescription and careful monitoring by a psychiatrist.\u000a- **Side effects vary** – Some may cause drowsiness, weight changes, or sexual dysfunction.\u000a- **Withdrawal risks** – Some medications (like benzodiazepines or SSRIs) should not be stopped abruptly.\u000a- **Therapy is key** – Medications are often most effective when combined with therapy (e.g., CBT).\u000a\u000aIf you or someone you know is struggling with anxiety or mental health concerns, **consult a psychiatrist or mental health professional** for personalized treatment. Would you like information on non-medication approaches for anxiety?'),'[{''title'': ''1mg: Coenzyme Q10 + L-Carnitine L-Tartrate + L-Arginine + Methylcobalamin + Lycopene'', ''url'': ''https://www.1mg.com/generics/coenzyme-q10-l-carnitine-l-tartrate-l-arginine-methylcobalamin-lycopene-510720'', ''content'': ''Uses: Coenzyme Q10+L-Carnitine L-Tartrate+L-Arginine+Methylcobalamin+Lycopene is used in the treatment of nutritional deficiencies . | Side Effects: Most side effects do not require any medical attention and disappear as your body adjusts to the medicine. Consult your doctor if they persist or if you’re worried about them Limited data available'', ''relevance_score'': 0.95}]','2026-07-29 08:19:49.078573');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(81,17,'user','summarize these reports.','','2026-07-29 08:29:59.588678');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(82,17,'assistant',unistr('I’d be happy to help summarize medical or health-related reports. Please share the key details or upload the reports (if possible), and let me know:\u000a\u000a1. **What type of reports are they?** (e.g., lab results, imaging, doctor’s notes, research studies)\u000a2. **What specific information do you need summarized?** (e.g., diagnosis, treatment plan, key findings)\u000a3. **Any focus areas?** (e.g., symptoms, medications, follow-up steps)\u000a\u000aFor example:\u000a- *"Summarize my blood test report focusing on cholesterol and glucose levels."*\u000a- *"What are the key findings from this MRI scan of my knee?"*\u000a\u000a**Reminder:** I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional for interpretation or concerns.'),'','2026-07-29 08:30:06.247874');
