ys are normal in size. * Renal veins are normal. Bilateral kidneys are showing prompt, synchronous excretion. No evidence of calculus / hydronephrosis. . . « Surface is smooth. e Perinephric spaces are normal. PELVIS o Urinary bladder appears partially distended. co Prostate appears normal in size and attenuation. PERITONEAL CAVITY & OMENTUM Ill-defined omental haziness with nodular enhancement seen involving infrarenal region in anterior aspect. Associated peritoneal thickening noted. Moderate ascites noted. No evidence of appendicitis. ¢ No evidence of adrenal mass lesion. ¢ Bones appear normal. Visualized soft tissues appear normal. . ¢ Abdominal wall normal. IMPRESSION: 1. Significant diffuse circumferential wall thickening involving the distal esophagus, OG junction, fundus, and body of the stomach (up to 26 rr with associated perigastric fat stranding. Findings are highly suspicion malignancy.'', ''relevance_score'': 0.6804832674219243, ''source'': ''image:WhatsApp Image 2026-07-29 at 12.44.30.jpeg''}]','2026-07-29 08:38:00.879077');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(95,20,'user','180/100 is my BP. What is 100 and is it normal if not what is the cure','','2026-07-29 08:39:29.471878');
INSERT INTO messages VALUES(96,20,'assistant',unistr('Your blood pressure reading of **180/100 mmHg** is **not normal** and falls into the **hypertensive crisis** category (systolic ≥180 **or** diastolic ≥120). Here’s what the numbers mean:\u000a\u000a- **180 (systolic)**: Pressure in your arteries when your heart beats (pumps blood).\u000a- **100 (diastolic)**: Pressure in your arteries when your heart rests between beats.\u000a\u000a### **Is 100 Diastolic Normal?**\u000aNo. A diastolic pressure of **100 mmHg** is **Stage 2 hypertension** (diastolic ≥90). Combined with a systolic of 180, this is a **medical emergency** if accompanied by symptoms like:\u000a- Severe headache\u000a- Chest pain\u000a- Shortness of breath\u000a- Vision changes\u000a- Confusion or dizziness\u000a\u000a### **What to Do Now:**\u000a1. **Check again in 5 minutes** – If still ≥180/100, **seek immediate medical attention** (this could indicate organ damage risk).\u000a2. **If no symptoms**, contact your doctor **today** for evaluation and treatment.\u000a3. **Do NOT wait** – Untreated high BP can lead to stroke, heart attack, or kidney damage.\u000a\u000a### **Treatment (Under Medical Supervision):**\u000a- **Lifestyle changes** (DASH diet, low sodium, exercise, weight loss, stress management).\u000a- **Medications** (e.g., ACE inhibitors, calcium channel blockers, diuretics).\u000a- **Monitor BP regularly** at home.\u000a\u000a*I am an AI assistant for informational support only. Not a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional immediately for high blood pressure.*'),'[{''title'': ''hypertension'', ''content'': ''# Hypertension (High Blood Pressure) ## What is Hypertension? Hypertension, also known as high blood pressure, is a long-term medical condition in which the blood pressure in the arteries is persistently elevated. High blood pressure typically does not cause symptoms. Long-term high blood pressure, however, is a major risk factor for stroke, heart attack, heart failure, peripheral arterial disease, vision loss, chronic kidney disease, and dementia. ## Blood Pressure Categories ### Normal Blood Pressure - Systolic: less than 120 mmHg - Diastolic: less than 80 mmHg ### Elevated Blood Pressure - Systolic: 120-129 mmHg - Diastolic: less than 80 mmHg ### High Blood Pressure Stage 1 - Systolic: 130-139 mmHg - Diastolic: 80-89 mmHg ### High Blood Pressure Stage 2 - Systolic: 140 or higher mmHg - Diastolic: 90 or higher mmHg ### Hypertensive Crisis - Systolic: higher than 180 mmHg - Diastolic: higher than 120 mmHg ## Symptoms Hypertension is often called the "silent killer" because it usually has no symptoms. Some people with very high blood pressure may experience: - Headaches - Shortness of breath - Nosebleeds - Dizziness - Chest pain - Visual changes - Blood in urine ## Risk Factors - Age (risk increases with age) - Family history of hypertension - Being overweight or obese - Lack of physical activity - High sodium diet - Low potassium diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Stress - Chronic kidney disease - Sleep apnea - Certain medications (NSAIDs, birth control pills, decongestants) ## Management ### Lifestyle Modifications - DASH diet (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) - Reduce sodium intake to less than 2,300 mg per day - Regular aerobic exercise (30 minutes most days) - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Limit alcohol to 1 drink per day for women, 2 for men - Quit smoking - Manage stress through relaxation techniques - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) ### Medications - ACE inhibitors (lisinopril, enalapril) - ARBs (losartan, valsartan) - Calcium channel blockers (amlodipine, nifedipine) - Thiazide diuretics (hydrochlorothiazide) - Beta-blockers (metoprolol, atenolol) ## Complications of Untreated Hypertension - Heart attack or heart failure - Stroke - Kidney damage or failure - Vision loss - Peripheral artery disease - Vascular dementia - Hypertensive emergency ## When to Seek Medical Attention If your blood pressure reading is 180/120 mmHg or higher, wait five minutes and test again. If it is still elevated, contact your doctor immediately. A blood pressure reading this high can lead to a stroke or organ damage.'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7818280647193581, ''source'': ''hypertension.md''}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lower blood pressure) - Beta-blockers (reduce heart workload) - Antiplatelet agents (prevent blood clots) - Diuretics (reduce fluid buildup) - Blood thinners (prevent clots) ### Surgical Procedures - Angioplasty and stent placement - Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) - Heart valve repair or replacement - Heart transplant (in severe cases) ## When to Seek Emergency Care Call emergency services immediately if you experience: - Chest pain lasting more than a few minutes - Severe shortness of breath - Fainting or loss of consciousness - Rapid or irregular heartbeat with dizziness - Sudden severe weakness or numbness - Sudden difficulty speaking", ''relevance_score'': 0.7737284018163755, ''source'': ''heart_disease.md''}, {''title'': ''heart_disease'', ''content'': "# Heart Disease - Overview ## What is Heart Disease? Heart disease, also known as cardiovascular disease, refers to conditions that involve narrowed or blocked blood vessels that can lead to a heart attack, chest pain (angina), or stroke. It is the leading cause of death worldwide. Common heart diseases include coronary artery disease, heart failure, arrhythmias, and valve problems. ## Types of Heart Disease ### Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) The most common type of heart disease. CAD occurs when the coronary arteries become narrowed or blocked due to plaque buildup (atherosclerosis). This reduces blood flow to the heart muscle and can lead to heart attack. ### Heart Failure Heart failure means the heart cannot pump blood as well as it should. Despite the name, it does not mean the heart has stopped working. It means the heart is not pumping as well as the body needs. ### Arrhythmias Arrhythmias are irregular heartbeats. The heart may beat too quickly (tachycardia), too slowly (bradycardia), or with an irregular pattern. ### Heart Valve Disease When one or more heart valves do not work properly, the heart must work harder to pump blood through the body. ## Risk Factors ### Non-Modifiable Risk Factors - Age (men over 45, women over 55) - Gender (men at higher risk at younger ages) - Family history of heart disease - Race/ethnicity (higher risk for certain populations) ### Modifiable Risk Factors - High blood pressure (hypertension) - High cholesterol - Diabetes - Obesity (BMI > 30) - Smoking - Physical inactivity - Unhealthy diet - Excessive alcohol consumption - Chronic stress ## Warning Signs ### Heart Attack Symptoms - Chest pain or discomfort (pressure, squeezing, fullness) - Pain in one or both arms, the back, neck, jaw, or stomach - Shortness of breath - Cold sweat, nausea, lightheadedness - Women may experience atypical symptoms: unusual fatigue, indigestion-like discomfort ### Heart Failure Symptoms - Shortness of breath during activity or when lying down - Fatigue and weakness - Swelling in legs, ankles, and feet - Rapid or irregular heartbeat - Reduced ability to exercise - Persistent cough or wheezing - Swelling of abdomen - Rapid weight gain from fluid retention ## Diagnosis - Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) - Echocardiogram - Stress test - Cardiac catheterization - Blood tests (troponin, BNP, cholesterol) - Coronary angiogram - CT scan or MRI ## Prevention ### Diet - Heart-healthy diet (DASH or Mediterranean) - Limit saturated fat to less than 10% of calories - Limit sodium to less than 2,300 mg/day - Eat plenty of fruits, vegetables, whole grains - Choose lean proteins - Include omega-3 rich foods ### Exercise - At least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week - Or 75 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity per week - Muscle-strengthening activities 2+ days per week ### Lifestyle - Don''t smoke or quit smoking - Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9) - Manage stress - Get adequate sleep (7-9 hours) - Limit alcohol consumption - Manage diabetes, high blood pressure, and cholesterol ## Treatment ### Medications - Statins (lower cholesterol) - ACE inhibitors or ARBs", ''relevance_score'': 0.7513814006571706, ''source'': ''heart_disease.md''}, {''title'': ''first_aid_basics'', ''content'': ''## Allergic Reactions (Anaphylaxis) ### Symptoms - Swelling of face, lips, throat - Difficulty breathing - Hives or rash - Rapid pulse - Dizziness or fainting - Nausea or vomiting ### Treatment 1. Use epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen) if available 2. Call 911 immediately 3. Lay the person down, elevate legs 4. Give second dose after 5-15 minutes if no improvement 5. Monitor breathing and pulse ## Heat-Related Illnesses ### Heat Exhaustion - Heavy sweating, weakness, cold/pale/clammy skin - Move to cool place, loosen clothing, apply cool cloths - Sip water slowly - Seek medical attention if vomiting occurs ### Heat Stroke (Medical Emergency) - High body temperature (103°F or higher), hot/red/dry skin - Rapid pulse, confusion, unconsciousness - Call 911 immediately - Move to cooler environment - Cool body rapidly with cold water or ice packs - Do NOT give fluids if unconscious ## When NOT to Move Someone - Suspected spinal injury - Head, neck, or back pain - Arm or leg numbness - Fractures - Loss of consciousness without airway problems'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7503064074652841, ''source'': ''first_aid_basics.md''}, {''title'': ''diabetes_overview'', ''content'': "glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL - HbA1c target: less than 7% ### Medication Treatment may include oral medications (metformin, sulfonylureas, etc.) and/or insulin therapy, depending on the type and severity of diabetes. ## Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes - Cardiovascular disease (heart attack, stroke) - Neuropathy (nerve damage) - Nephropathy (kidney damage) - Retinopathy (eye damage, potential blindness) - Foot damage (risk of amputation) - Skin conditions - Hearing impairment - Alzheimer''s disease", ''relevance_score'': 0.7433019906830493, ''source'': ''diabetes_overview.md''}, {''title'': ''images-2'', ''content'': ''Patient Information Form Name: Date: Wendi your problem stat?; __escibe Probe Cause of Current Problem: (D caracdent Cl Woskinuy CO Gaduatonset other Did this Problem require Surgery: No Cl Yes 0 YesDateot surgery ast Medical History Oo youhaveaisry fhe fosoning problems? 2 Breathing Pblems D Seok 2 Depression Pregnant 1D BonajeintPeblems Bowed C1 Heat mebiems 1D Kidney Prabiems Wir then aol we G CanentWoundskinPrebems ©) Galbaddener Og ute Pacemaker 1 ectrclimpant © smoing 1 Tmoucancer sity atch: Headaches C Oisbete 1D Seep Apnea 1D NoSurgeries SugeniesMospeaations ver Complains 1D NoMedicaton Medications Peselt Medations that you ae taking eda) Dove FawsonorMeseaton TT No Keown allergies Allergies latex Ove Ome Todine Ove Ne Bromine Ws Cl no other Do you have anyreigiousicltrlvews that wil affect your treatment?” No Over ‘tional commentieading or Memory Problem) Signature bate'', ''relevance_score'': 0.7376969925666872, ''source'': ''image:images-2.png''}, {''title'': ''images-2'', ''content'': ''Patient Information Form Name: Date: Wendi your problem stat?; __escibe Probe Cause of Current Problem: (D caracdent Cl Woskinuy CO Gaduatonset other Did this Problem require Surgery: No Cl Yes 0 YesDateot surgery ast Medical History Oo youhaveaisry fhe fosoning problems? 2 Breathing Pblems D Seok 2 Depression Pregnant 1D BonajeintPeblems Bowed C1 Heat mebiems 